const STORY={start:"p0",chapters:[],scenes:{"p0": {"id": "p0", "c": 0, "title": "The Question", "p": ["Before the first kingdom was raised out of the mud, before the first spell was spoken aloud and swallowed by the dark, before humanity had learned to fear whatever waited beyond the edge of its firelight, there was a question.", "It was asked in a silence so complete that the silence itself seemed to listen.", "~“What happens when something that cannot be destroyed is imprisoned?”", "The first person to ask it was a king, and he asked it too late.", "His name was Odalric, and he ruled the river-city of Marrowmere, which was the richest place in the young world. One evening a stranger walked in through the Sunward Gate, which had been shut for nine hundred years, and the guards who should have stopped him found that they were smiling and could not remember why.", "The stranger was tall and tidy and delighted with everything. He admired the king’s throne. He admired the king’s daughter, who was ill, and whom he promised to cure before breakfast. He said that his name was Kael Veyrin, that he was only passing through, and that he wished to make the king a very small offer.", "“Ten perfect harvests,” said Kael Veyrin. “A cure for the little one. A wall no army could climb. And in exchange, Your Majesty, merely a trifle: the Sunward Gate, opened from the inside, by your own hand, tonight.”", "The king, who loved his daughter, opened the Gate.", "“Wonderful!” cried Kael Veyrin, and he applauded. He truly did. “Magnificently done. I have never seen it opened so politely.”", "By morning, Marrowmere was ash. The harvests came, ten of them, each one perfect, and nobody was left to eat them. The princess was cured, and she walked alone through the burning streets looking for someone to tell. And when the king, in chains, with his crown melted into his hair, asked how a man could be so cruel, Kael Veyrin looked honestly wounded.", "“Cruel? But I kept every word of it,” he said. “It is hardly my fault that you weren’t listening to the words.”", "He was not cruel the way a storm is cruel. He was cruel the way a host is attentive. He remembered what you loved, and he brought it to you on a tray.", "Empires fell before him. Heroes came, and he complimented their swordsmanship while they bled. Kings sold him their borders for a joke, a prophecy, a lie they were already half inclined to believe. And always he was smiling, and always he was courteous, until the rare moment when the smile went out, and then even the mountains looked away.", "At last the gods came down.", "They came under the one who led them, Tavrion the Stormcrowned, who held the lightning in his fist the way a farmer holds a rope. They were the oldest powers in creation, and they fought Kael Veyrin for a year and a day. The sky split. The sea stood up on its hind legs. And when it was over, Tavrion knelt in the ruin of a hundred battlefields with his crown of storm gone gray, and understood what the others had understood a long time before him.", "They could not kill him.", "Nothing they possessed could. He could be broken, and scattered, and burned to the last cinder, and he would stand up out of the smoke dusting off his coat. “Truly,” he said, almost kindly, “you have all been awfully brave. It is the nicest thing about you.”", "The gods considered it for an age. Then they answered the question the only way gods know how: with a sacrifice.", "They gathered every drop of divine power they had ever held and poured it into a single being, until nothing was left of them but memory and a great and ringing silence. And the power took shape, in the heavens above the broken world, as a dragon of living blue fire.", "They called it the Azure Dragon. It was the bridge between the mortal world and the world of spirits, the gods’ last breath and their strongest word.", "It fought Kael Veyrin for forty days and forty nights, and on the forty-first it defeated him.", "It could not destroy him. But it bound him, with the last of the gods’ light, into a seal of such a depth that even he, who could talk a king out of his own gate, could not find a door in it. They say that as the seal closed he was laughing. They say his last words were courteous.", "“Do give my regards to eternity. I shall be back before it notices I’ve gone.”", "For a dragon is not a lock. A dragon grows old. So the Azure Dragon made the only promise it could, and bound itself to it: in every age, it would choose one human being to carry its power, and when that human died, it would wake again in a newborn child, and the guardian would begin once more. A chain of lives, one after another, standing watch over a sleeper who was only waiting.", "Six times the Azure Dragon woke in a human child. Six times that child grew up, stood between the world and the dark, and was lost to it.", "Of the seventh, nothing was ever written.", "Empires rose and drowned. The old world folded itself away behind a curtain, and ordinary people forgot it had ever been there, and went on living, ignorant and warm, above a secret they had no idea they were keeping.", "Then, on a night when every star was torn out of the sky at once, as though a hand had passed across the heavens and wiped the slate, the seal cracked.", "The sound was too low for any ear to hear. Dogs lay down in their yards. Birds dropped from the wires like fruit. In a thousand cellars, a thousand bottles of wine turned to vinegar at the same instant. And far beneath the earth, in a place older than stone, something that had slept since before the first dawn opened its eyes.", "They were not the eyes of anything that had a name.", "~“…Found you.” It sounded delighted.", "The stars came back, one by one, trembling like sparks blown from a dying fire. And in a cold room above a shop that had closed for the night, in a town that nobody would remember, a child was born.", "Nobody came for him. In the morning there was a blanket, a note with a single word on it, and a very surprised nurse.", "Down the street, every dog in the town began, at the same moment, to howl at a sky that was perfectly empty."], "nx": "c1s1"}, "c1s1": {"id": "c1s1", "c": 1, "title": "Gerald", "p": ["Arlo Pruitt had worked out that his bedroom was exactly the size of a walk-in closet, mostly because it had been one.", "The Pruitts called it the junk room. It had a window the size of a tea tray, a bed he had to climb into from the foot, and a wall that was warm on one side and cold on the other, because the furnace lived behind it. The furnace was called Gerald. Arlo had named him in the first week, for company, and Gerald had responded by clanking, sighing and occasionally producing a noise like a very old man being told a joke he didn’t understand. They had got on well from the start.", "“Morning, Gerald,” said Arlo, to the wall.", "Gerald groaned.", "“Yes. Me too.”", "It was six-forty, and raining, and he was eight years old, and a month ago he had had a birthday. Nobody had mentioned it, which was fine. He had not mentioned it either, because that would have been like asking for something, and Arlo was careful about asking. It was the reason he kept a notebook under his mattress with a page headed THINGS THAT ARE NOT MY FAULT. The list was long. It was in alphabetical order, because he liked things to be in order when so few things were. Under B: Birthday (forgotten). Under L: Lunch money (Cody). Under R: Rain.", "He did not write down the dream. That was in another notebook, a smaller one, with a green cover, and he looked at it now with the guilty fondness of someone checking on a secret.", "It had come again in the night. It always came. A forest, deeper and greener than any forest he had ever seen, with trees so tall that the sky was only a rumor at the top of them and a path that wandered off between the roots, patient, as if it expected him. There was no wind, and every leaf moved anyway. There was a light in the distance, a pale, cool blue, like the light under the sea. And there was a sound. It was not a word. It was the sound that a person makes when they are trying to say your name and are not quite sure how it goes.", "He had started drawing it in the spring. He was not good at drawing. The trees all leaned the same way, and the path looked like a dropped noodle. But he had drawn it forty-one times now, and every time he got a little closer, and it frightened him that he could tell.", "He drew it again, quickly, with the stub of pencil he kept behind the radiator. Then he put the notebook back, climbed out over the end of the bed, and pulled on yesterday’s sweater, which smelled only slightly of yesterday. In the dark glass of the window he caught sight of himself: a small brown boy with dark curls no comb had ever beaten, and eyes that always looked faintly apologetic about being there.", "There was a damp green smell in the room, like a garden after rain. He frowned at it. He opened the window the width of a hand to check, and the smell was not outside.", "“Right,” he said, to Gerald. “That’s new.”", "Gerald, for once, said nothing at all."], "nx": "c1s2"}, "c1s2": {"id": "c1s2", "c": 1, "title": "The Good Cereal", "p": ["The pruitts lived at 14 Thornbury Court, in a house exactly like all the others in the close, apart from the extra smell of floor polish and the sound of a television that had never been turned off in living memory.", "Denise Pruitt was in the kitchen, already dressed, doing three things at once with the brisk, wounded efficiency of a woman who had been doing three things at once since 1998 and wanted it known. She was not a monster. Arlo had thought about this a great deal. She did not hit, or shout, and she signed everything the school sent home. She simply had a way of looking at him as if he were an appliance that had arrived without instructions and could not be returned.", "“Not that one,” she said, without turning round, as he reached for the cereal. “That’s Cody’s. There’s the other box.”", "The other box was bran. It was always the other box.", "“Sorry,” said Arlo.", "He said it to the cereal, to the chair, to the kettle he knocked with his elbow. He had noticed a while ago that he apologized to furniture. It was not a habit he was proud of.", "Gary Pruitt sat at the table behind a wall of newspaper, producing small noises of disapproval for the news. Cody, eleven, large for his age and shaped like a younger Gary, was working through a bowl of the good cereal with the careful concentration of a boy who knew he was being watched and intended to enjoy it.", "“Spare’s up,” Cody remarked.", "“Cody,” said Denise, in the voice that meant she had no intention of doing anything about it.", "“What? It’s his name. He’s the spare.”", "This was a joke that had begun with Gary, a year ago, when the social worker had left. Well, we’ve got our spare now. It had been meant, probably, as a joke about the spare room. It had stuck the way those things do. By now Arlo had stopped being able to tell whether the others had forgotten what it meant or whether that was exactly why they kept saying it.", "“There’s a form,” said Arlo, carefully. “For the trip. It needs signing.”", "Denise sighed. It was a very large sigh, loud enough to be a short speech. She signed the slip without reading it, and gave it back to him between two fingers, as a person hands over something that might be sticky.", "“Your social worker’s coming Thursday,” she said. “So I’d rather you didn’t go telling her stories.”", "“I don’t tell stories.”", "“Last time you said you were hungry.”", "“I was hungry,” said Arlo, before he could stop himself.", "The kitchen went very quiet. Gary’s newspaper lowered an inch. Arlo watched the three of them look at him, and felt the tiny cold click that always came when he had said something true in a room that did not want it.", "“There’s toast,” said Denise at last, in a voice like a closing door. “It’s yesterday’s. Eat it before you go, and don’t drip.”", "He ate it standing up. It was fine. It had butter on it, in the sense that a butter knife had once been in the same room.", "He was at the front door, with his bag, when he realized that the damp green smell had followed him downstairs. It was very faint. It was a smell of wet leaves and moss and cold stone.", "He sniffed his sleeve. He sniffed his hand.", "It was coming from his pocket.", "He put his fingers in, cautiously, and drew out the pencil stub from behind the radiator. Wound round it, as if it had grown there overnight, was a single thin green tendril, with one small leaf on the end.", "It was alive. It was warm.", "Arlo stood in the hall of 14 Thornbury Court, staring at it, while behind him in the kitchen Cody asked, with his mouth full, whether the Spare had gone yet.", "Very slowly, he put the pencil back in his pocket, and went out into the rain."], "nx": "c1s3"}, "c1s3": {"id": "c1s3", "c": 1, "title": "Sir Biscuit", "p": ["Harlow road primary was a low brown building that looked like it had been assembled by someone who had heard of schools but never seen one.", "It had a playground with a painted hopscotch grid, a wall for kicking balls against, and a patch of wet grass that was out of bounds from October to April for reasons nobody could explain. Arlo walked in through the gate with his head slightly down and his shoulders slightly forward, which was how he arrived everywhere. He had calculated, over time, that it made him eleven per cent less likely to be noticed. He was aware that this was probably not a real number. But a boy has to have something.", "The thing about being overlooked, he had found, was that you saw a great deal. He saw that Mrs. Kessler in the office always ate her cookies in the second drawer so the principal would not catch her. He saw that Dexter Crowe laughed a half-second before the others did, to make sure they would. He saw that the new girl, June, who had arrived three weeks ago in a coat too big for her, stood at the very edge of every room, with her back to a wall and her eyes everywhere, like someone who was used to checking the exits.", "He had not spoken to her. She had not spoken to him. They had, he privately felt, an understanding.", "“Right, everyone,” said Mr. Alvarez. “Coats off. Bags away. And nobody, please, panic.”", "Which was, of course, how you knew.", "Mr. Alvarez was a tall, tired man with a bow tie he wore out of defiance, and he was the only teacher at Harlow Road who learned the names of children who didn’t put their hands up. He stood at the front of Room 4B holding an empty glass tank, with sawdust on his pants, wearing the expression of a man who has lost something he was personally responsible for.", "“Sir Biscuit,” he said, “has gone.”", "There was a gasp. Sir Biscuit was the class hamster, a round golden creature with an air of unearned authority, and he had been there for longer than most of the children. A girl at the back began to cry. Dexter Crowe, in the second row, immediately announced that he had seen a fox.", "“We’re in the middle of a town, Dexter,” said Mr. Alvarez.", "“A very small fox.”", "Arlo had not meant to say anything. It was simply that he was looking at the tank, and the tank was wrong. There was a gap behind the water bottle where the sawdust had been dug out in a neat tunnel, and the tunnel went toward the wall. The wall, he realized, had a pipe running along the base of it.", "“Sir?” he said, in what he meant as a normal voice and came out as a mouse’s.", "“Arlo?”", "“Hamsters like warm places. And dark ones.” He swallowed. “And it’s cold today. So the heating’s on. And the pipe goes along the wall and into the closet where the recycling is, and that’s the warmest closet, because it’s next to the furnace room.” He stopped. Thirty faces had turned toward him, and he wished he had said nothing, and wished the carpet would eat him. “That’s only what I’d do,” he added. “If I was small.”", "There was a short silence.", "“Well,” said Mr. Alvarez slowly. “It’s as good a theory as I’ve got.”", "They found him in the recycling closet, curled up on top of a mountain of shredded paper beside the warm pipe, looking supremely satisfied with himself. Arlo had to wriggle in on his stomach to reach him, because the door was small and everyone else was too big or too frightened of spiders. When he came out with a hamster cupped in both hands, dusty and slightly ashamed, a cheer went up that was the loudest sound he had ever heard anyone make on his account.", "It lasted about four seconds.", "“I saw him go in first,” said Dexter Crowe, loudly. “I was just about to get him.”", "“Were you, Dexter?” said Mr. Alvarez, in the careful voice of a man who suspected he was not being told the truth and had nowhere to put the suspicion.", "“Yeah. And then Pruitt crawled in front.”", "“Well,” said Mr. Alvarez, after a pause that was entirely too long. “Thank you, both. Thank you, everyone. Biscuit’s safe, and that’s what matters.” He looked as if he wanted to say more. Then thirty children began to talk at once and the moment closed. He gave out gold stickers to the people who had helped. Dexter got one. It was, Arlo reflected, a very nice sticker. It had a star on it.", "“Fine,” said Arlo, to himself, to the wall. “Stickers expire anyway.”", "He had never heard of a sticker expiring. It simply felt like the sort of thing that must be true.", "He had only just sat down, with his ears hot and dust in his hair, when he felt someone looking at him. He glanced up. Across the room, at the back, with her chin on her fist and a book open in front of her that she was clearly not reading, June Wren was watching him with her head very slightly on one side.", "It was not the look the others gave him. It was not pity. She was looking at him, he thought, the way you look at a puzzle that has just revealed it has an extra piece.", "Then she looked back down at her book."], "nx": "c1s4"}, "c1s4": {"id": "c1s4", "c": 1, "title": "The Girl Who Said Give It Back", "p": ["It happened at break, behind the wall where the recess monitor couldn’t see, which was where it always happened.", "Arlo had been trying to be invisible. He was sitting on the cold concrete with his back to the bricks and his green notebook open on his knee, and he was drawing the forest. It was the forty-second time. He had finally got the trees right. They did not lean all one way, after all; they leaned toward the path, as if listening. He was lost in it. He had not heard the footsteps.", "“Hey, Spare.”", "A shadow fell across the page.", "Dexter Crowe was a broad pink boy with a fixed smile, who gave the impression of having been assembled from sausages. Behind him stood the Fitch twins, Marcus and Jasper, who had never in their lives been seen to disagree with Dexter or each other. Dexter had a gift. He could look at a person for three seconds and work out, exactly, what they were most afraid of losing. And then he would take it.", "“What’s that?”", "“Nothing.”", "“It’s something.” He plucked the notebook out of Arlo’s hands in one easy movement, and flipped through it. His lips began to move. “‘Dream, March the fourteenth.’ ‘Dream, March the fifteenth.’ Oh, Pruitt. You’ve got a diary.” A delighted pause. “You’ve got a diary about trees.”", "“Give it back.”", "“‘The light was blue and the path was waiting.’” Dexter read it in a high wobbly voice, to the twins’ delight. “Aw. Spare’s got a boyfriend in the woods.”", "“Give it back, Dexter.”", "“Make me.”", "This was the point, Arlo knew, at which a braver person would have done something. He did what he always did. He stood up, and opened his mouth, and let his terrible, rotten, unstoppable brain come up with the best possible sentence.", "“That was the worst impression of a person reading I’ve ever heard,” he said. “You sounded like a goose being asked about its mortgage.”", "There was a pause.", "Marcus Fitch laughed, a sharp involuntary snort, and then looked horrified. Dexter’s smile, which had never in eight years moved from its position, went entirely still.", "“Right,” said Dexter softly.", "It was a mistake, and Arlo knew it, in the same instant that the twins took hold of his arms. He fought, in the sense that a wet sock fights when picked up. Dexter walked across the yard with the notebook, unhurried, to the metal trash can by the wall, and held it above the open top. Something small and cold went through Arlo’s chest. It was not the notebook that he was afraid for, exactly. It was that in there, in forty-one pages of bad drawings, was the only thing in the world that was his.", "“Please don’t.”", "“Ooh,” said Dexter. “He said it nicely.”", "“Please—”", "“Give it back.”", "It was not shouted. That was what he noticed afterward. It was said quite quietly, in a flat, clear, slightly bored voice, by someone standing about six feet behind Dexter with her hands in the pockets of a coat that was too big for her.", "June Wren did not look angry. She looked like somebody who was waiting for a bus.", "“Mind your business, new girl,” said Dexter.", "“It is my business. You’re being loud. I was reading.” She tilted her head. “Give him his book. Then go away. It’s a very simple plan and I’ve thought about it quite hard.”", "“And if I don’t?”", "“Then I’ll have to do something about it,” said June, with a kind of weary regret, “and I really would prefer not to. I’m very new here. I’m trying to be good.”", "Dexter laughed. The twins laughed. Arlo could feel his heart going like a washing machine. And Dexter, who was a bully, which is a different thing from being a fool, looked at the small girl in the coat, and decided that it would be very funny to drop the notebook in the trash can right in front of her.", "“Oops,” he said, and opened his hand.", "And, quite suddenly, he was on the floor.", "It happened so fast that Arlo was not sure he had seen it. One moment Dexter was standing at the trash can. The next his two feet had gone out from under him as if someone had pulled a rug, and he went down flat on his back with a thump that knocked the smile off his face and most of the air out of his chest. The notebook slapped onto the concrete beside him.", "Everyone looked at his feet.", "His shoelaces had been tied together. Not clumsily. They were in a neat, small, perfect double bow, a bow so tidy that it looked as if it had been done by a very patient person in a very good light.", "“Gravity,” said June Wren, mildly. “It’s such a bully.”", "Arlo looked at her. She had not moved. She had been six feet away the whole time, hands in her pockets. He was entirely sure of it.", "“What did you—” Dexter was scrambling up, scarlet, flailing at his own feet. “What did you do? Fitch! Get her!”", "The twins did not move. They were both, Arlo noticed, staring at the neat double bow with the expression of boys who had suddenly remembered some other, more urgent engagement.", "“Right,” said a voice, loudly. “What’s all this?”", "It was Mrs. Garvey, the recess monitor, a short furious woman with a whistle. She had a gift for arriving at the exact moment when the evidence would be worst. She saw Dexter on the ground. She saw June, standing over him with her hands in her pockets. She saw a small boy with grass on his knees, and a notebook on the floor.", "“Juniper Wren,” she said grimly. “I should have known. A new girl, three weeks, and already—”", "“She didn’t do anything.”", "Arlo had no idea who had said it. It was a thin, high, determined little voice, a voice with a crack in the middle. He realized, in a slow dawning horror, that it was his own.", "“I— He’s lying. Dexter took my book. He was going to put it in the trash can. And he fell over because— because his laces were undone. He tripped. She was only standing there.” His ears were burning. His voice went up at the end, like a question. He could feel every person in the playground looking at him. “That’s what happened. I saw it.”", "Mrs. Garvey looked at him as one looks at a hamster that has begun to give a speech.", "“Is that right, Dexter?”", "“It’s— she—” Dexter, flustered, was still wrestling with his shoes, and did not seem able to say that he had been felled by a girl in a coat. “It’s not— He’s making it up.”", "“It’s a very good bow,” said Mrs. Garvey, after a moment, staring at his feet.", "She did not look as if she believed anyone. But she was tired, and it was cold, and in the end she sighed and said that she would be watching the lot of them and that Dexter had better get his shoes fixed before he broke his neck. The twins helped him. Dexter limped past, and as he went by Arlo he bent down, very close, and said in a small, flat, genuinely frightening voice:", "“Later, Spare.”", "Then they were gone, and the whistle blew, and the playground rearranged itself around them as water does around a stone.", "Arlo bent, with shaking hands, and picked up the notebook."], "nx": "c1s5"}, "c1s5": {"id": "c1s5", "c": 1, "title": "Half a Sandwich", "p": ["She found him by the bike shed after school.", "He had gone there to wait for Cody to cycle off, because walking home alone ten minutes behind Cody was better than walking home with him. It was raining again, a thin gray drizzle, and he was sitting on the damp concrete step with his knees drawn up, trying to work out what he had done.", "He had spoken up. He had actually said, out loud, in front of a teacher, she didn’t do anything. He had never, in the whole of his life, said such a thing for someone else. He was not at all sure it counted as courage. It had felt a good deal more like being sick.", "“You’re shaking,” said June Wren.", "He jumped. She was standing at the corner of the shed, still in the big coat, with her backpack over one shoulder and a squashed sandwich in a bag. She did not come closer. She stood at the same distance she had stood from Dexter, as if she had decided how near was polite.", "“I’m cold,” said Arlo.", "“No you’re not.”", "“I’m a bit cold.”", "“You’re a bit terrified. I think it’s quite sensible.” She considered the sandwich, and then without any ceremony she broke it in half and held one half out. “It’s turkey and pickle. Mom makes it too thick. It’s sort of a hazard.”", "He looked at it. He could not remember the last time anybody had given him food that was not left over. He did not know the rules. It seemed to him there ought to have been rules.", "“Why did you do that?” he said.", "“Give you a sandwich?”", "“Back there. With Dexter.”", "“Oh.” She sat down on the step, a careful distance away, and took a large bite of her own half. “It was boring watching. I’m bad at boring.”", "“That’s not a reason.”", "“It’s a reason.” She chewed. Her face, as she looked at the rain, changed a little; the brisk composure slipped, and under it was something else, quieter and a good deal older. “And because somebody didn’t, once. For someone I know. And I’ve never really stopped being mad about it.”", "He did not ask. It seemed to him that the sentence had a door in it and that she had left it ajar on purpose, and he knew very well what it was to want a door left ajar.", "“You tied his laces,” said Arlo.", "“No I didn’t.”", "“You were six feet away.”", "“I know. It’s a terribly difficult knot at that distance.” She said it to the rain, quite straight-faced. “Quite impressive, really.”", "“June.”", "“Hm?”", "“How did you do it?”", "She turned and looked at him then, properly, for the first time. She had a thin serious face, with a sharp chin and a great deal of dark hair that kept escaping from its braid, and very light gray eyes that did not, he noticed, blink as often as other people’s. She had a smear of pencil on her cheek. She looked like someone deciding something that mattered.", "“Can I see the notebook?” she said instead.", "“What?”", "“The green one. The one he was going to put in the trash can.” She held out her hand. “I won’t laugh. I promise on all the things I like.”", "He did not know why he gave it to her. It was a thing nobody had ever been allowed to hold. He gave it to her with both hands, like a thing that might break, and watched her open it on her knee, and turn the pages, slowly, one at a time. The forty-one drawings. The path like a noodle. The trees leaning in, listening. The little pale scribble at the end of every one, that was meant to be a light.", "She stopped turning.", "It was a small change. If he had not been a boy who had spent his life watching other people’s faces for weather, he would never have seen it. But the color went out of her cheeks, quite slowly, as if someone had turned a tap. Her hand, resting on the page, began very faintly to tremble.", "“Where did you draw this from?” she said. Her voice had gone odd and flat.", "“Nowhere. It’s a dream.”", "“How long?”", "“Since I was— I don’t know. Little. Five?” He swallowed. “It’s getting clearer. It used to be just green, and now there’s a path. And a light at the end.”", "“What color is the light?”", "“Blue.”", "She shut her eyes.", "“And does it,” she said, very carefully, as if she were crossing a river on stones, “ever say anything?”", "“It doesn’t— It’s not words. It’s like someone trying to say my name. And they keep getting it a bit wrong.” He laughed, uncertainly. “That sounds mad. It’s a bit mad, isn’t it?”", "“No.”", "“Are you sure?”", "“No,” said June Wren. She opened her eyes, and there was something very bright and very frightened in them, and she was looking at him as if he were a window that had suddenly turned out to look onto the sea. “I mean no, it isn’t mad. I mean—” She stopped. She closed the notebook, gently, and put it back in his hands. “Arlo. Has anything happened? Anything odd. In the last few days. Anything you couldn’t explain?”", "He thought of the smell of wet leaves. He thought of the little green tendril round a pencil stub, and the warmth of it in his palm.", "He had never told anyone anything true that did not get him into trouble.", "“Maybe,” he said.", "“Don’t tell me yet,” she said at once, and held up her hand. “Not here. Not— I have to think. And I have to talk to someone.” She was already getting up, brushing crumbs off her coat. “Don’t tell anyone else. Not Mr. Alvarez, not your foster mom, nobody. Promise.”", "“How do you know about my foster mom?”", "She hesitated. “It’s a small school,” she said. She did not look at him as she said it.", "“June—”", "“I’ll see you tomorrow. Eat the sandwich. It’s getting damp.” She slung her bag onto her shoulder and walked off across the playground, quite fast, a small figure in a big coat, with the rain running down her braid. At the corner of the building she stopped, and turned.", "“Arlo,” she said.", "“Yes?”", "“What you did, in the playground. Saying that. It was very brave.” She said it quite simply, as one might hand over a coin. “I wanted you to know that somebody saw.”", "Then she was gone.", "He sat for a long time on the wet step with the sandwich in his hand. It was lumpy, and thick, and slightly flattened, and the pickle had gone through the bread. He ate all of it. It was the best thing he had ever tasted."], "nx": "c1s6"}, "c1s6": {"id": "c1s6", "c": 1, "title": "The Voice in the Green", "p": ["That night, the dream was different.", "He knew it at once. He was standing on the path. That in itself was new, because usually he only watched; this time he could feel the moss under his bare feet, cold and wet and slightly springy, and the air on his skin, smelling of rain and pine and something older. The trees stood on either side, tall as cathedrals, all leaning toward him, and now he could hear what they were doing. They were whispering. It was not a language. It was the sound of a hundred thousand leaves all trying very quietly to tell him something at once.", "The blue light was nearer. It lay at the end of the path like a pool of cold water, and it was pulsing, slowly, like breath.", "He took a step toward it.", "“Oh, there you are.”", "Arlo froze.", "It was a man’s voice. It was warm, and amused, and exquisitely courteous, a voice like honey stirred into hot tea, and it came from nowhere and from everywhere, from the trees and the moss and the tops of his ears. There was no menace in it. That was the strangest part. It sounded like a person who had been looking for something for a very long time and had found it, and was trying quite hard not to be too pleased.", "“Don’t be alarmed,” said the voice. “I’m terribly pleasant. Everybody says so. Well. They did.” A soft, delighted laugh. “It’s a mess in here, I do apologize. The dark is mostly decoration.”", "“Who are you?” Arlo whispered. His mouth had gone dry.", "“A friend. A very old one. You wouldn’t remember me, but I do so remember you.” A pause. “Or someone very like you. You’ve her chin, you know. Isn’t that funny?”", "The trees whispered more urgently. The blue light flickered.", "“I’ve a great deal to tell you, Arlo,” said the voice, and it was the sound of his name that made the hair stand up on his arms, because it had said it perfectly. It was the first time anyone had ever said it right. “I’ll be brief. I’m told it’s my worst quality, and I try to disappoint people. But first, I should like you to do one small thing. It’s only a trifle.”", "“What?”", "“Come a little closer. I can’t quite see you.”", "Arlo looked at the path. He looked at the light. He had spent his whole life wanting someone to say it like that, as if he were a thing worth coming closer for, and every part of him that was lonely, which was most of him, wanted to take the next step.", "And a very small, cold, sensible voice at the back of his head, which sounded a good deal like June Wren, said: Don’t tell anyone else."], "nx": "c1s6b"}, "c1s6b": {"id": "c1s6b", "c": 1, "title": "The Voice in the Green", "p": ["“No, thank you,” said Arlo.", "There was a pause.", "“I beg your pardon?”", "“I said no, thank you.” His voice shook, but it came out. “I’m not supposed to talk to people I don’t know. Everybody says. It’s—” he scrabbled for it—“a rule.”", "For a moment there was only the whisper of the leaves.", "Then the voice laughed. It was a rich, round, genuinely delighted laugh, like a man who has been dealt an unexpectedly good hand, and it went on for rather longer than was comfortable.", "“Oh, that is marvelous,” said the voice. “A rule! Oh, I do like you. You’re going to be such fun.” The laugh softened. The warmth did not go out of the voice, and that, somehow, was what made Arlo’s knees go weak. “Never mind. I’m a patient man. I’ve been patient for a very long time. I can wait until you’re ready.”", "The blue light dimmed.", "“Sleep well, Arlo,” said the voice, kindly. “And do say hello to your friend with the clever shoelaces.”", "He woke with a gasp.", "It was dark, and raining, and Gerald was groaning gently behind the wall, like an old dog in its sleep. His heart was beating so hard that he could feel it in his teeth. For a long time he lay with his eyes wide open, staring at the ceiling, trying to make himself believe it had been a dream.", "His pillow was damp. He put up his hand.", "There were leaves on it. A scatter of them, small and green, still wet, as if someone had stood over him in the dark and shaken a branch. They were warm. And as he held one up to the thin gray light from the tea-tray window, it curled slowly around his finger, the way a hand closes round another hand.", "Outside, on the street, a car went by. Its headlights swept across the wall and went out.", "Arlo lay very still, with the leaf wrapped round his finger and the dreadful, shining word going round and round in his head.", "Friend. He had told nobody about her. Nobody at all."], "nx": "c2s1"}, "c2s1": {"id": "c2s1", "c": 2, "title": "Bartholomew", "p": ["The leaf had survived the night, which Arlo had not expected, and had grown a second leaf, which Arlo had expected even less.", "It sat in the lid of an old pencil tin on the windowsill, where he had put it at three in the morning in a state of great confusion, along with a pinch of damp soil from the plant pot downstairs and a spoonful of tap water, because he had no idea what else leaves wanted. In the thin gray light it looked entirely ordinary, apart from the part where it turned, slowly, to face him as he sat up.", "“Don’t,” said Arlo.", "The leaf did not stop. It leaned further, like a sunflower, like a dog that has heard the word walk.", "“I can’t keep you,” he told it, with what he hoped was firmness. “I’m not allowed pets. I had a goldfish at my last place and it went missing within a week, and they said it was natural causes, and I’ve always had my doubts.” He paused. “You’re not going to eat anything, are you?”", "The leaf waved a little. It was a bit like being greeted by a very small, very enthusiastic hand.", "Arlo put his face in his own hands. “Great,” he said. “Thanks, Gerald. Really. Nothing at all to say about this?”", "The wall gave a long, noncommittal clank.", "He named it Bartholomew, in the end, because it seemed like a Bartholomew. It was a leaf with the personality of a golden retriever and no concept at all of the word discreet. He had spent the first hour of the morning trying to hide it. In the sock drawer, it had pushed the sock aside and peered out. In his backpack, it had climbed up the zip. In the end he had settled on the pencil tin, wedged into the very bottom of his bag under his reading book and a half-eaten packet of mints, and he had told it, severely, to be a leaf.", "It had considered this. Then it had gone very still, and very flat, and exactly the color of a leaf.", "“Good,” said Arlo, startled. “That’s— yes. Well done.”", "He had spent the walk down the stairs rehearsing. He had eleven ways to say I have a plant that is alive in a way that plants should not be. None of them sounded like anything a sane person would say to a girl he had known for one sandwich.", "Downstairs, the television was on. It was always on. The morning news was explaining, with great gravity, that it was going to be raining.", "“Don’t just stand there like a coat-stand,” said Denise, not turning round. “Your PE kit’s on the radiator. It’s a bit damp. I didn’t get to it.”", "It was not a bit damp. It was the exact squelching texture of a drowned animal. But there was a rule at 14 Thornbury Court, never stated and always understood, that the only appropriate response to a small unkindness was thank you, and Arlo had become very practiced at saying it.", "“Thanks, Mrs. Pruitt.”", "“And it’s Thursday tomorrow,” she went on briskly. “Ms. Patterson is coming at four, so I’ll want you in and tidy. Tidy, Arlo. And no stories.”", "“I don’t—”", "“And don’t tell her about the hamster thing. Cody says you’ve been showing off.”", "“I wasn’t. It was just a—”", "“Cody says.”", "It was not worth it. It was never worth it. Arlo ate his bran standing up, with the particular dull speed of a boy who has learned that the kitchen is a place you leave as quickly as you can.", "He had very nearly made it to the front door when Cody, who missed nothing that could be used against someone, put out one large foot and hooked his backpack off his shoulder.", "“What’s in here, then, Spare?”", "“Nothing. Give it back.”", "“It’s heavy. You’re hiding something.” Cody unzipped it with the unhurried air of a man opening a very interesting present. Out came the reading book. Out came the mints. And out, rattling, came the pencil tin. “Ooh. What’s this?”", "Every drop of blood went out of Arlo’s face.", "Cody prised up the lid. For a moment, Arlo saw a single small flat leaf, curled and brown and quite dead, lying at the bottom of the tin with a crumb of soil, exactly like something that had been in there for a month.", "“Ew,” said Cody, in disgust. “It’s a weed. You’re keeping weeds.”", "“It’s— it’s for science.”", "“It’s for being weird.” He tossed the tin back. It hit the floor, and the lid rolled away, and Arlo scooped it up with trembling hands and stuffed it into his bag, and heard, deep in the darkness of the tin, a very faint, very smug rustle.", "“Thank you,” he whispered to it in the hall, once he was outside and the front door had closed. “You were amazing.”", "The leaf, in the bottom of his bag, was as quiet as a thought.", "But he thought it was probably pleased."], "nx": "c2s2"}, "c2s2": {"id": "c2s2", "c": 2, "title": "The Rules of June Wren", "p": ["June was waiting for him at the school gate, with a face like a thundercloud and a clipboard.", "He had not known that anyone at Harlow Road owned a clipboard. It was a real one, with a metal clip and a school-issue worksheet attached, on the back of which, in tiny furious handwriting, she had written ARLO — QUESTIONS and underlined it twice.", "“You’re late,” she said.", "“It’s eight-forty.”", "“I’ve been here since seven-thirty.”", "“Why?”", "“Because I didn’t sleep, and I thought if I stood by a gate for long enough it might feel like I was doing something.” She thrust the clipboard under her arm. “I have twenty-three questions. We’ll do them at lunch, in the library, because nobody goes in the library and Mr. Dempsey sleeps. Don’t say anything now. Not here. People listen.”", "“Who listens?”", "“Everyone. It’s a school.”", "She stalked off. Arlo watched her go with the dim sense of having been given an order by someone who was in no way qualified to give it and had nonetheless done it extremely well.", "The morning lasted about nine years.", "He sat through math, and through a spelling test on which he spelled necessary three different ways, and through Mr. Alvarez’s lesson on the Romans, during which he drew a small, careful, entirely accidental picture of a leaf in the margin of his book and erased it at once with his heart pounding. Dexter Crowe had not so much as looked at him. This was worse, in a way. Dexter at the back of the room, with the Fitch twins either side like two bookends, was a thing like weather: a pressure that gathered, and did not break.", "At lunch, he went to the library.", "It was a small, dim, cheerful room that smelled of carpet and old paper, and in the corner, under a sign that said QUIET PLEASE and a second sign beneath it that said NO REALLY, sat Mr. Dempsey, a large round man with a soft gray beard, asleep with a copy of The Hobbit open on his stomach. He was snoring in a stately sort of way, like an elderly steamship.", "June had commandeered the table furthest from him. She had laid out two pencils, a rubber, a ruler and a packed lunch with the geometric exactness of a general laying out maps.", "“Sit,” she said.", "He sat.", "“Rule one,” said June. “Don’t tell anyone. That includes your foster family, your teacher, and your social worker, no matter how nice she is. Rule two: don’t touch anything that glows. Rule three—” She hesitated, and the clipboard drooped a little. “Rule three is, if anyone ever offers you something you want very badly, in exchange for something small, you say no. Always. Even if it sounds like the best thing you’ve ever heard.”", "Arlo thought about a warm voice in a forest, and the word trifle.", "“I already did that one,” he said.", "June’s pencil stopped.", "“What do you mean, you already did that one?”", "So he told her.", "He had meant to tell it carefully, in order. But once he began, it came out of him the way water comes out of a tipped bottle, all at once and in the wrong sequence: the path, and the moss under his feet, and the way the trees had leaned in to listen. The blue light. The voice like honey stirred into hot tea. He told her the line about the rule, and the laugh, and she did not move at all. He told her about the leaves on the pillow.", "And then, because it seemed to him that he ought to be honest, he told her the last thing.", "“It said to say hello to you.”", "“To me.”", "“To my friend with the clever shoelaces.” He swallowed. “It— I hadn’t told anybody I had a friend. I didn’t, until yesterday, so it wasn’t— I mean I hadn’t even—”", "“Stop,” said June.", "She had gone the color of a candle. She put her pencil down, very slowly, parallel to her eraser, and put both her hands flat on the table, as if she thought it might float away.", "“It said friend,” she said.", "“Yes.”", "“It knew I tied his laces.”", "“I think so.”", "“I did that from six feet away. I was extremely careful. I thought nobody was—” Her voice cracked in the middle, and she stopped, and for a moment she looked about eight years old, which she was, instead of a very small commanding general. “I wasn’t supposed to. I’m not allowed. I’ve got a list of things I’m not allowed to do at school and it’s a very long list and I did number four.”", "“What’s number four?”", "“Tying people’s shoes together.” She shut her eyes. “I wasn’t even good at it. That’s the humiliating part. It’s the only thing I can do well, and I’ve practiced it for six months, in my bedroom, on a teddy bear. I can’t do anything small. I’m good at the big loud things, and I’m terrible at the delicate ones, and everybody says that’s the problem with me.” She opened her eyes. “That bow took me from March.”", "“It was a really good bow.”", "“I know it was a good bow.” She sniffed fiercely. “That’s not the point.”", "“I just wanted you to know I thought so.”", "“Thank you,” said June, stiffly, and wiped her nose on her sleeve, and was visibly annoyed at herself for it.", "Mr. Dempsey gave a tremendous snore and turned a page of The Hobbit in his sleep.", "“Right,” said June, collecting herself, like a person picking up spilled marbles. “Question one. When you say the leaves were warm—”", "It was an extremely thorough interrogation. She asked him how long the dreams had lasted, and what time he woke, and whether there was a smell. She asked him if the light was the same blue as anything he had ever seen, and when he said it was a bit like the community pool, she wrote it down with great solemnity. She asked him if he had ever felt like someone was standing behind him when nobody was. She asked him about the Pruitts, in a careful voice, as if holding something sharp. Twice she said mm, in a way that made Arlo feel suddenly and unexpectedly watched over. And somewhere around question fourteen, when she asked whether anything had come with him from the dream, he said, “Do you want to see something? You’ll hate it,” and opened his bag.", "He put the pencil tin on the table, and took off the lid.", "“It’s called Bartholomew,” he said. “I know. I’m sorry.”", "The leaf unfolded.", "It did it all at once, with a small happy flourish, the way a cat stretches when it has been shut in a closet. It had three leaves now. A little twist of green tendril rose out of the soil and swayed, looking about, in the pale library light. And then it saw June, and stopped.", "“Oh,” said June, in a small voice.", "The tendril crept across the table. It did not go straight to her. It went nervously, in little hesitating stages, rather like a person approaching a very distinguished guest at a party. It reached her hand. It touched one finger, very lightly, and a single leaf curled round the tip of it, gentle as a handshake.", "June did not breathe.", "“It’s not a normal plant,” she said at last, in a shaky whisper.", "“Yes. I’d got that far.”", "“It’s— no, it’s, you don’t understand. It’s not a normal plant, Arlo. This isn’t a garden plant that got lucky. It’s the Tanglewood.”", "“The what?”", "“It’s—” She pulled her hand back, quite carefully, and Bartholomew, with an air of faint disappointment, retreated across the table and crawled back into the tin. “I’m not allowed to say. Not yet. I need Nana. She’s better at explaining things than I am. She’ll get mad if I explain it badly.” She hesitated. “She’ll get mad anyway. But less.”", "“Who’s Nana?”", "“My grandmother. She’s—” June tried several words in her mouth, and rejected them. “She’s very particular. She looks things up for people. She’s the only person I know who’s ever been right about everything, and she’s very rude about it.”", "Arlo took a long moment to look at the girl across the table. It was not unlike looking at the sun through a keyhole. In three weeks, he had never heard her say so many words at once. She had gone pink around the ears. She was holding a pencil like a dagger.", "“June?”", "“What?”", "“How did you know about my foster mom? Yesterday. You said it was a small school.”", "She was quiet for a while.", "“I didn’t,” she said at last. “Know, I mean. Not at first. I—” She looked at the clipboard, and then, with a great effort, at him. “Nana said there was a boy. At Harlow Road. She said he needed looking after, and she couldn’t say which one, because she only saw him from behind, and she saw a great deal of green. And I’ve been looking for three weeks, and I’ve been through every boy in four classes, and I’ve got a list, and you were number nineteen, because you never put your hand up.”", "“I’m—” Arlo’s throat did something. “You were told to look after me?”", "“No!” said June, scarlet. “I mean— at first. Technically. I was meant to find you. Not look after. It was a sort of assignment.” She dropped her voice. “And then you said that thing in the playground. You said she didn’t do anything, in front of Mrs. Garvey, with your voice all wobbly. And I stopped being assigned.”", "“You did?”", "“Yes. I’m not any more. I’m just—” She searched for it, with a frown. “Here. I’m just here. On purpose. That’s— it’s different.”", "Nobody had ever been here on purpose for Arlo before. He did not know what to do with the information. He put his hands under the table, where they could not do anything embarrassing, and looked hard at the ruler.", "“Would you,” said June, brusquely, “like to meet her? After school. Nana. She lives on Alder Lane, it’s ten minutes. It’s not a thing you’d have to tell your foster mom, I mean, she wouldn’t mind, I don’t think. People don’t, with Nana.”", "Arlo thought of the front door of 14 Thornbury Court, and the television, and a woman who would not notice if he came in at four or at nine.", "Nobody had ever invited him anywhere. Not even to a birthday party that he would not have been allowed to go to.", "“Yes,” he said, and was horrified to hear his voice crack. “Yes. Please. I mean, if that’s— yes.”", "“Good,” said June, with enormous dignity, and wrote something on her clipboard. “Question twenty-three, answered.”", "“What was it?”", "“Whether you’d say no to a stranger.” She crossed it off. “I’d hate it if you said yes.”"], "nx": "c2s3"}, "c2s3": {"id": "c2s3", "c": 2, "title": "The Corner", "p": ["Dexter crowe was waiting at the gate after school.", "It was exactly the sort of thing he would do. He leaned against the railings with his arms folded and his rings glinting, and on either side of him, like a pair of identical gargoyles, stood Marcus and Jasper Fitch. A little crowd had begun to gather at a safe distance, with the contented stillness of children who have sensed a fight and do not want to miss the beginning.", "“There he is,” said Dexter. “Spare. I did say later.”", "“It’s later,” said Marcus helpfully.", "“I know it’s later, Marcus. That’s what I said.”", "Arlo stopped. June stopped beside him. He felt her go absolutely still, in the way a cat goes still before it has decided to be a problem.", "“Let’s go round,” he whispered.", "“We’re not going round.”", "“There’s a back gate, we could—”", "“We are not,” said June, quite audibly, “going round a person who is wearing a ring on every single finger.” She turned her head. “It’s the rings that annoy me. I’m not sure what it’s for. Does he do the dishes in them?”", "Somebody in the crowd snorted. Dexter’s smile hardened.", "“You’re going to be so sorry, new girl.”", "“I’ve been sorry before. I thought I’d try a different feeling.”", "“Run?” whispered Arlo.", "“Yes,” said June, with relief. “Run.”", "They ran.", "It was the first time in his life that Arlo had run toward something. He had run away from things all his life, quite fast, in a sort of skittering crouch. This was different. His legs, to his surprise, simply went, and he heard the thunder of feet behind him and the shriek of Marcus Fitch, and June’s braid whipping past his shoulder, and over it all he heard a sound he did not at first recognize as himself. It was a kind of high, wild, hiccuping whoop.", "He was laughing.", "“Left!” shouted June. “Left, left, the next one’s left—”", "They shot across the parking lot of the laundromat and down a narrow passage between a pawn shop and an empty shop, so tight that he had to turn sideways, and then the light changed, and the noise of the road dropped away, and they tumbled out onto a lane.", "It was a very small lane. It was paved with gray cobbles worn smooth as soap, and it ran slightly uphill between two rows of crooked houses leaning toward one another, like women gossiping across a fence. The street lamps were old iron ones. There was a cat on a windowsill. The whole place smelled of rain, and woodsmoke, and, for no reason he could name, hot buttered toast.", "Arlo had lived in this town for a year. He had not known it was here.", "“Don’t stop,” gasped June. “Not yet. Look behind you.”", "He looked.", "The three boys had reached the end of the passage. They were fifteen feet away, in a cluster, panting, and Dexter, in the lead, had one foot lifted to come in after them. He put it down. Then he took it up again. It was the strangest thing: it was as if there were a pane of glass across the mouth of the lane, and he had found it with his nose.", "“What—” said Dexter. He frowned. He put out his hand, and patted at the air in front of him, with a baffled expression, like a man trying to find the light-switch in someone else’s house. “What was I—”", "“Dex?” said Jasper.", "“I was going to— I had a—” Dexter turned in a slow, puzzled circle. He looked at his hands, at the rings, at the twins. “Where are we?”", "“Dunno,” said Marcus.", "“It’s a bit boring round here,” Jasper offered.", "“Yeah.” Dexter scratched his head. “Let’s go get fries.”", "They turned round and wandered off, quite peacefully, back the way they had come. In a moment, there was nobody there at all.", "Arlo stood at the top of the cobbled lane with his chest heaving and his mouth wide open.", "“What,” he said, “was that?”", "“The lane’s a bit shy,” said June.", "“A bit shy?”", "“It doesn’t like to be looked for. It has a— well.” She was bright red from running, and still, he could not help noticing, trying very hard not to look pleased with herself. “It’s a sort of a tidy thing. It makes people forget they were coming. It only works on the people who don’t belong, though. Obviously. It’s not a trick, it’s just very polite.” She stopped. “That was supposed to be a secret.”", "“Was it?”", "“Quite a big one, actually.”", "Arlo looked back at the empty mouth of the passage, and the cat on the windowsill, and the leaning houses with their hot-toast smell. He thought about the shape of a pane of glass he could not see, and how he had walked through it without even noticing, and what that might mean.", "He did not say anything. He thought that she knew.", "“Come on,” said June, not looking at him. “Nana’s at the end. The one with the fish.”"], "nx": "c2s4"}, "c2s4": {"id": "c2s4", "c": 2, "title": "Alder Lane", "p": ["The house at the end of Alder Lane was above a seafood shack.", "It was an old seafood shack, long closed, with a sign that said HADDOCK & SON, SEAFOOD, FRIES, CHOWDER, in letters that had once been red and were now the color of tired brick. The windows were shuttered. There was a faded picture of a smiling cod, with a chef’s hat, and somebody had added a mustache. Beside the shop, a narrow blue door with a brass knocker in the shape of an acorn gave onto a steep staircase, and at the top of it, through a second door that stood a little open, came the click and clack of knitting needles.", "“Now,” said June, with her hand on the banister, in the voice of a person giving a safety briefing. “She’s going to say a lot of things. Most of them will be rude. Some will be about me. You’re to ignore those. She likes to be dramatic, and she’s a great one for lying down on the carpet in the middle of a conversation, and if she tells you she’s a hundred and forty, she isn’t, she’s seventy-nine. And whatever you see in the corner—”", "“What’s in the corner?”", "“Nothing. Don’t look at it. It gets self-conscious.”", "Arlo went up the stairs.", "It was the first room in the world that he ever wanted to stay in. He knew it at once, with the same cold clarity with which he knew the rules of a chess game he’d never played. It was low and crowded and warm. The ceiling beams were hung with bunches of dried herbs and small brass bells and a surprising number of umbrellas. There were clocks on every wall. There were twenty-three at least, and they all told different times, and none of them looked as if it were wrong. There were jars on every shelf, labeled in a spidery hand: Mist (Tuesday). Four O’Clock (Sunday). Left-Handed Wind. A fat orange cat the size of a footstool sat on a pile of cushions, looking at Arlo with the weary contempt of a creature that has seen everything.", "And in a wingback armchair, with a half-knitted scarf in her lap and an expression of faint, alert amusement, sat the smallest old woman he had ever seen.", "She was about the size of a large child. Her hair was white and stood out from her head in a dandelion cloud, and she wore a green cardigan with a great many pockets, and spectacles with two different lenses. One was a deep violet. The other was amber. Over the top of them, her eyes, which were a very bright, very sharp, very ordinary brown, looked at him in complete delight, as one looks at a Christmas present that has arrived on time.", "In the corner, behind her chair, a second pair of knitting needles was working steadily away at a long blue sock. There was nobody holding them.", "“Don’t look at it,” whispered June.", "Arlo looked at it. The needles faltered, and went a little pink, and began, with elaborate casualness, to hum.", "“Well,” said the old woman. “There you are.”", "He did not know what to say. In his experience, strangers either ignored him or told him to move. Nobody had ever said there you are as if they meant it.", "“Hello,” he said. “I’m Arlo. I’m sorry about the— about coming in. June said it was all right. I can go.”", "“Don’t you dare. Sit.” She patted the cushion beside the cat, and the cat gave her a look of the deepest betrayal, and moved four inches. “I’m Odette Wren. Nana, to anyone who’s ever lost an argument with me, and most of the people I’ve met are in that category. You’ll have noticed I’m small. It was a tactical decision. It makes people underestimate me, and then I’m terribly pleased.”", "“She’s not a hundred and forty,” said June.", "“I may be a hundred and forty. It depends who’s counting.”", "“You told the man at the post office you were a hundred and forty.”", "“He was being impertinent about my parcel.” Nana Wren looked at Arlo and her eyes crinkled. “Now. You’ll want tea. Everyone wants tea. It’s the first thing a person says to themselves in a crisis. There’s a pot by the fire.”", "There was a pot by the fire: a large, round, brown, battered teapot with a chipped spout and a lid that did not quite fit. As Arlo looked at it, it gave a little shiver, rose a clear inch from the hearth, and floated across the room. It paused politely beside his knee. Then it tipped, and a stream of hot amber tea poured itself into a cup that had appeared, quite on its own, in the air beneath the spout.", "Arlo watched it fill. He watched it stop, one drop short of the brim, with great delicacy.", "“Thank you,” he said faintly, to the pot.", "“Oh, you’re polite,” said Nana Wren, to June, with deep satisfaction. “He talks to the teapot. You said nothing about that.”", "“Nana!” June had gone the color of a beetroot. “You can’t just— He doesn’t know yet! There’s a way you’re meant to start these things, there’s a whole, there are steps, I had a list, I was going to explain the thing about the— Nana, you’ve made the tea walk!”", "“It didn’t walk. It drifted. There’s a difference.” The old woman waved a hand. “He’s seen worse. He saw you tie that boy’s laces.”", "“I did that very discreetly.”", "“You were six feet from a trash can, dear, in the middle of a playground, on a Tuesday. A deaf man would have noticed.”", "Arlo held the cup in both hands. It was warm, and it smelled of cinnamon and rain. He looked at the floating teapot, and at the knitting needles in the corner, which were now doing a complicated, self-conscious thing with a heel, and at the girl beside him, who had her face in her hands.", "He was eight, and a little frightened, and a good deal more interested than anything he had ever been in his life.", "“Please,” he said, “is it magic?”", "There was a short pause.", "“Well, that’s a very direct question,” said Nana Wren, with a pleased gleam. “Not hello, how do you do, is that a talking cat. Straight to the point. I like him, June. I’m keeping him.”", "“It’s not a talking cat,” said June.", "“I do not talk,” said the cat, with great dignity, “to people who have not been introduced.”", "Arlo dropped his tea.", "It did not hit the floor. The cup, at a flick of the old woman’s fingers, hung in the air a few inches from the rug, with the tea still in it, quite level, like a thing that had been told to wait. Arlo stared at it. He stared at the cat, who had gone back to washing one paw with an expression of tired superiority. He stared at the floating tea.", "“Is it?” he whispered.", "“Is it what, sweetheart?”", "“Magic.”", "“Yes,” said Nana Wren. And, more softly, in a voice that stripped the joke out of her face and left only the warm, steady, tremendous seriousness underneath: “Yes, Arlo. It is. I’m sorry to say it so quickly. I’d meant to take longer, but the world seems to be in a hurry, and I’m afraid it’s been a long time coming. It’s all real. All of it. And I’m a witch, and June’s a witch, and the cat is a cat of considerable opinions.”", "“A witch in training,” said June, in a small voice. “Level two.”", "“Level two,” said Nana, “with a gift for shoelaces.”", "He could not decide whether to cry or whether to laugh. He thought he might do both. There was a very peculiar feeling in his chest, as if some small, shut, forgotten room had been opened and aired. For eight years, the world had been exactly as large as a junk room. And now, sitting on a cushion beside an offended cat, he had the dizzy, enormous, shining sense that the walls were not the walls at all, and that on the far side of them there was a great deal more room than he had ever been told.", "“Why didn’t anyone say?” he asked. His voice came out very small. “Why didn’t anyone tell me?”", "“Because most of you aren’t supposed to know, honey. It’s a rule.” Nana Wren’s face did something complicated. “And because, I think, there was somebody who would have found out if you did.”", "“Who?”", "She looked at him over the violet and amber lenses, and for the first time since he had come into the room, she did not seem like a funny old woman at all. She seemed like a very old, very tired lighthouse that has been looking out to sea for a long time and has just seen something on the horizon.", "“I’m going to ask you for something, Arlo,” she said. “It’s a bit of nerve. But I’d be very grateful if you’d show me the leaf.”"], "nx": "c2s5"}, "c2s5": {"id": "c2s5", "c": 2, "title": "The Reading", "p": ["He put the pencil tin on the little round table between them, and took off the lid.", "Bartholomew rose from the soil like a sleepy child out of a bed. He had four leaves now, and a tendril as long as a pencil, and he stood straight up with his small green head tilted, and looked at Nana Wren. And Nana Wren, who had a great many pockets, took a magnifying glass out of one of them, and looked back.", "It was a very long look. Arlo, watching, saw the humor drain slowly out of her face, bit by bit, like water out of a basin. Her lips moved. The amber lens caught the lamplight. And she put out one small crooked finger, and the leaf, very formally, wrapped itself around it and bowed.", "“Oh, you dear thing,” said Nana Wren, in a voice quite different from her usual one. “You’ve come such a long way.”", "“It’s from the Tanglewood,” said June. Her voice was tight. “Isn’t it? I thought it was. I can tell it’s, it hasn’t got the right smell for here, it’s too green, it’s—”", "“It is a good deal more than that, sweet pea.” Nana laid down the magnifying glass. “Tell me again, Arlo, what the dream is.”", "He told it for the third time that day. It was easier now. He saw how she listened, with her head tilted and her hands folded, and every so often her eyes flicked to June, who was sitting very still on the arm of the sofa with her knees drawn up, and then back to him. When he got to the voice, she closed her eyes.", "“And he said,” said Nana, softly, when he had finished, “to say hello to June.”", "“Yes.”", "“How very like him.”", "The room had gone quiet. In the corner, the knitting needles had stopped. One by one, the twenty-three clocks seemed to be ticking a little more loudly than before.", "“Nana,” said June, very low. “You know who it is.”", "“I have an idea. I have had an idea since last Tuesday, when I saw a green boy in my tea.” The old woman opened her eyes. “I should like to be wrong. I should like it very much. And there’s only one way to find out, and I’m afraid it’s a bit unpleasant.”", "“What is it?”", "“I want to follow the thread.”", "“What thread?” said Arlo.", "“The one that runs from you to the forest. Every dream has one, you see. It’s like a piece of wool, only invisible, and it comes out of you and goes off into the dark, and at the other end is the thing that’s been calling.” She said it with a little wry smile, as if it were nothing at all. “I only pull on it very gently. I only look. Like peering down a well. And whatever’s at the other end can’t possibly know I’m there.”", "“Is it dangerous?” said Arlo.", "“No,” said Nana Wren.", "June made a small noise.", "“Very little,” said Nana Wren, with perfect firmness. “It’s a perfectly sensible precaution. I’ve done it a hundred times. I once found Mrs. Peterson’s missing hearing aid by following the thread of her cat’s mind. I’ve done a very good deal of dreadful things, and I’m still here. June, my dear. The bowl.”", "June got up as though she had been given a sentence. She fetched a wide shallow bowl of white china from a shelf, and filled it from a copper jug that poured, with a small happy noise, from three feet above it. She placed it in the middle of the table. The water was perfectly clear. It did not even ripple.", "“Close the curtains,” said Nana.", "The curtains closed. They did it quite on their own, with a soft swish, and the room went dim. In the corner, a candle lit itself. It was a small white candle with a blue flame.", "“Give me your hand, Arlo. The right one. And June, sweetheart, you take the other. Not too tight. I’m not made of china. Whatever you see, whatever you hear, you’re not to let go and you’re not to say anything until I do. Do you understand?”", "“Yes,” whispered Arlo.", "“And Arlo.”", "“Yes?”", "“Don’t be frightened.” She smiled, quite suddenly, at him over the blue flame, a wide, bright, wicked smile that showed one chipped tooth. “I’m a very good witch. Between you and me, I’m the best in the street.”", "She took his hand.", "It was very small and very dry, and it was warm. She closed her eyes. For a time, nothing happened. The candle burned. The clocks ticked. The water in the bowl lay black and still, a little pool of dark with the blue flame at the heart of it.", "Then, slowly, the surface began to glow.", "It was faint at first, a thin green shimmer, like light under a leaf. It deepened. In it, very small and very far away, Arlo saw the forest. There was the path, the dropped noodle, the trees leaning in. He could even see the moss. He could have drawn it. It was every line of the forty-two drawings, and it was alive, and it was exactly as he had known it must be, and he felt the beginnings of something large and strange rise in his chest.", "“Oh,” breathed Nana Wren. “Oh, how lovely. It’s a proper one.”", "The water showed the path winding off between the roots. It showed the blue light at the end of it, brighter now, pulsing like a heart.", "“I’m going closer,” said Nana. Her voice had gone far away. “Hold on, children. There’s a— it’s very thick. There’s a good deal of dark. And a— oh. There’s a gap. How curious. There’s a door in the trees. A great round—”", "She stopped.", "Her hand tightened on his.", "It was only a tiny movement, no more than a flinch. But he felt it go through her whole body, and he opened his eyes and saw that her face had changed. The wicked, amused brightness had gone out of it like a lamp. The deep lines about her mouth had gone hard. Her eyes, behind her closed lids, were moving rapidly from side to side, and her lips were moving.", "“No,” whispered Nana Wren.", "The candle flame bent.", "It did it slowly, as if some great unseen mouth had breathed on it. It lay down flat and blue, and then it stood up again, and the room was suddenly very cold. All the clocks stopped.", "All of them, on the same second.", "In the sudden silence, Arlo heard the sound of his own breath, and June’s, quick and shallow, and the tiny, terrible sound of the old woman’s heart beating in her wrist.", "“No,” said Nana Wren again, a little louder. “No, it isn’t. It can’t be. You’re sealed. I have read every line of that seal. Oh, you wicked, wicked—”", "In the bowl, the blue light had changed.", "It was not the pulse of a heart any more. It was the color of a very deep sea. It was rising up in the middle of the water in a slow curl, like smoke, and out of the smoke, quite distinctly, Arlo saw the shape of a face. It was a long, handsome, amused face, with a pair of cool pale eyes and a smile that came a very little way, and no further, up one side of the mouth.", "He knew it. He had never seen it before in his life, and he knew it. He knew it the way you know a smell you’ve only smelled in a dream.", "The face tilted its head.", "“Odette Wren,” said the voice.", "It was no longer far away. It came from the bowl, and from the candle, and from the clocks, and from the warm dry hand in Arlo’s. It was the voice of the forest. It was warm and rich and utterly delighted, and it filled the little room as steam fills a kitchen.", "“What an absolute pleasure,” it said. “I knew your great-grandmother, you know. Every bit as rude, and exactly your chin. I did say your family would be fun.”", "“June,” said Nana Wren, in a thin and perfectly steady voice. She had not opened her eyes. Her fingers were crushing Arlo’s. “June, sweetheart. When I say so. You’re going to take him and—”", "“No, no, no,” said the voice, with gentle reproach, and the water rippled as though someone had put a finger in it. “Don’t spoil it. You’ve only just got here. I haven’t even been introduced.” The cool eyes in the bowl moved, slowly, and turned. They turned right through the dim room, past the old woman and the frightened girl, and they stopped, with the gentleness of a man arriving at a long-awaited guest, on Arlo.", "“There you are,” said the voice, with enormous affection. “Hello again, my dear. I did say I was patient.”", "Arlo could not move. He could not breathe. He could feel every hair stand up on his arms, and some small, cold, steady part of him, a part that had grown up in a closet with the furnace, said very clearly: It knows your name.", "“Arlo,” said Nana Wren, quite calmly, with her eyes tight shut. “Sweetheart. Listen to me. Did he tell you his name?”", "“No,” Arlo whispered.", "“Good.” Her voice shook for the first time. “Good boy. Don’t ask him for it.”", "“Oh, but it’s such a nice name,” said the voice, sadly.", "Every light in the room went out.", "It was not a gradual thing. One moment there was a blue candle, and a bowl, and a handful of clocks that had stopped. The next, there was a darkness so total that Arlo could not see his hand in front of his face, and in the darkness, close to his ear, so close that he could feel the breath of it, somebody laughed.", "“Do carry on, Odette,” said Kael Veyrin, very softly. “I’ll just come in.”"], "nx": "c3s1"}, "c3s1": {"id": "c3s1", "c": 3, "title": "Four O’Clock (Sunday)", "p": ["The darkness lasted exactly as long as it took Nana Wren to lose her temper.", "“Humphrey,” she said.", "It was not loud. It was said the way a person says a name when she has been carrying it in her pocket for a long time for exactly this occasion. Arlo felt her hand tighten around his, small and dry and stronger than a hand had any right to be, and from somewhere near the floor, in the black, a voice answered with deep and weary dignity.", "“I am aware.”", "“The door.”", "“I am aware, Odette. I was aware before you were. I have been aware since the candle went blue, and I should like it noted for the record that I said, on Tuesday, that your green boy would be trouble.”", "“Noted. Door.”", "There was a sound like a large, orange, offended cat springing across a room, followed by the unmistakable thud of a body landing against a door and a sharp, businesslike click. Then, far down the stairs, every bolt on the street door slid home at once, and something heavy, which Arlo thought might be a chest of drawers, dragged itself across the landing and wedged against it.", "“June,” said Nana. “Light.”", "“I can’t,” said June. Her voice shook. “It won’t, Nana, I tried, there’s something in the, it’s all—”", "“Don’t try. You’re better at the loud things. Be loud.”", "There was a short, tense silence.", "Then June Wren said, in a voice that was much smaller and a good deal more furious than Arlo had ever heard it, “Fine,” and the room lit up like a lightning strike.", "It did not look like a candle. It looked like a miniature sun that had been shut in a drawer and had finally got out. It exploded into being at the center of the ceiling, hot and white and shrieking, and every shadow in the room fled for the corners. The jars on the shelves rattled. The umbrellas on the beams flew open all at once. Humphrey the cat, who was crouched on the sill with his ears flat and every hair on his body standing straight out like a bottle brush, said, “Not that loud,” and ducked.", "“That,” said Nana Wren, “is my granddaughter.” She sounded, even then, a little pleased. “You’ll have to forgive her. She hasn’t any subtlety.”", "“Sorry,” said June.", "“Don’t you dare apologize. Look.”", "They looked.", "The room was exactly as it had been, and it was entirely different. The curtains were still drawn. The twenty-three clocks hung on the walls with their hands stopped, every one of them at the same minute. The bowl of water sat on the round table. The candle, with its small blue flame, burned on in the corner like a very patient eye.", "But everything was leaning.", "It was very slight. It was only the way grass leans when something large is moving through it a long way off. Every shadow in the room, every dark patch under the shelves and behind the chairs and inside the jars, had turned a few degrees, in perfect silence, toward the table.", "Toward the bowl.", "“He’s in the water,” whispered Arlo.", "“He’s in the thread,” said Nana. She had let go of his hand at last, quite gently, and risen from her chair. She was suddenly very much taller than she had been. It was a trick of the way she stood. “I looked down a well, my dears, and he looked back up it, and now it’s a very straight line between my kitchen and wherever he is. That’s what a reading is. It’s a door you hold open with your own hand.” She rolled up the sleeves of her green cardigan. “I should have known better. I did know better. And I looked anyway, because I’m an old fool who wanted to be right.”", "“Nana—”", "“Shush. Humphrey. The Sunday.”", "“Which Sunday?”", "“The four o’clock.”", "“Odette, that’s the good Sunday.”", "“Humphrey, I will not ask you again.”", "With the air of a creature who had been asked to give up his last sardine, Humphrey the cat leapt onto a high shelf, put out a paw, and delicately knocked a small brown jar off the edge. It fell for a very long time. It fell, in fact, for rather longer than a jar should. And Nana Wren, without so much as looking, caught it neatly in one hand, thumbed off the lid, and flung the contents across the room.", "It was only light.", "It was a long gold slanting shaft of it, thick as honey, the light of an ordinary Sunday afternoon in an ordinary kitchen, when the sun comes low through the window and lies across the floor and there is nowhere in the world you would rather be. It poured out of the jar and landed across the table and the bowl and the black, leaning shadows. And where it touched, the shadows slowed. They did not retreat. They simply remembered, it seemed, that it was a Sunday, and that there was no particular hurry.", "“That,” said Nana, a little out of breath, “won’t hold him. Nothing will hold him. But it will make him a bit sleepy. It’s the cheapest trick I know, and it’s done more for me than all the fancy ones put together.” She looked at the children. “You’ll want to remember that. The most useful magic is hardly ever the loud kind.”", "“You just told me to be loud,” said June, with a wobble.", "“That’s because you’re terrible at the other kind. One has to work with what one has.”", "In the bowl, the black water was rising."], "nx": "c3s2"}, "c3s2": {"id": "c3s2", "c": 3, "title": "The Man in the Steam", "p": ["He came up out of the bowl like steam out of a kettle, and he took his time about it.", "It was not a body. Arlo knew that at once, the way he knew that the face in a dream was not a real face. It was a shape, a faint white swirl that gathered itself out of the air above the table and stood. It had shoulders, and a long neat coat the color of old ink, and a head that was a little to one side, and it was so exactly like a man that for a moment Arlo forgot to be afraid. It was very tall, and very tidy. It had pale eyes. It was smiling.", "“Forgive me,” said Kael Veyrin. “I’m not quite all here. It’s a bit of a long way to come, and I’m afraid I left most of myself in the basement.” He looked round the room with warm and unconcealed interest. “Odette, what a charming place. The jars! I do so admire a woman who labels things.”", "“Get out of my house,” said Nana Wren.", "“I’m not in your house, my dear. I’m in your thread. It’s a subtle distinction, but I believe it’s rather important.” He bent, with delight, and examined a brown jar on the shelf. “‘Left-Handed Wind.’ How perfectly lovely. And I see the cat’s still alive! Humphrey. What a distinguished fellow. You were a kitten when I saw you last.”", "“I was never,” said Humphrey, from the top of the bookcase, “a kitten.”", "“Oh, but you were. You were adorable.”", "“I will have your eyes.”", "“I’m sure you’d try.” Kael Veyrin straightened, and turned, quite slowly, and his smile went round the room, past the old woman and the cat and the furious, shaking girl with the light blazing over her head, and landed on Arlo like a hand on a shoulder. “And there he is. Hello again. Don’t get up.”", "Arlo had not been going to. His legs had gone to water. He was sitting on a cushion, in a room that smelled of cinnamon and thunderstorms, and a man who was not there was looking at him with an expression of the most extraordinary affection.", "“I did say I’d come in,” said Kael, apologetically. “I’m so sorry about the dark. It’s a dramatic habit. I picked it up in the Third Century.”", "“You’re not coming in,” said Arlo. His voice was a thread. “I said no.”", "“You did. It was the most delightful thing. I’ve been telling everybody.” He tilted his head. “But I’m not here for that, you see. I’m not here to take anything. That’s the funny part.” He spread his hands, the picture of an honest man at a market. “I’m here to give.”", "“Don’t,” said June. “Arlo. Rule three.”", "“Oh, the little one remembers her rules. How I do admire a girl with a clipboard.” Kael did not look at her. He was looking at Arlo, steadily, and the air in the room seemed to thicken around his voice, so that every word he said came to Arlo with a strange soft weight, like a hand held out in the dark. “Arlo. I’d like to tell you something that nobody has ever told you. I’d like to tell you about your mother.”", "The room went very still.", "Arlo was aware of his own heart. It was a small stupid animal in a cage, and it had begun to run in a circle. He had thought about this, once. He had thought about it a thousand times, on a thousand nights in a bed that was the size of a cupboard, with a furnace groaning in the wall. He had made her a face. He had made her a voice, a coat, a way of laughing, and he had put them all away in a drawer in the back of his head so that nobody could find them and tell him they were silly.", "“You don’t know who my mom is,” he whispered.", "“I know exactly who she is,” said Kael Veyrin, gently. “I know her name. I know what she was wearing. I know the color of the blanket she wrapped you in, and I know what she said when she put you down, and I know why she never came back. It’s a lovely story, my dear. It will break your heart.” A pause. A tender, small, terrible pause. “And I would be delighted to tell it. Simply for the asking.”", "“Arlo,” said Nana Wren. “Don’t.”", "“What’s the catch?” said Arlo.", "It came out sharp. He was a little surprised by it himself. He had spent his whole life with people who gave him things, a stale slice of toast, a hand-me-down jacket, a pat on the head, and every single one of them had had a catch. He knew the shape of it, the way you know the shape of a step in the dark. It was like a small cold stone in the middle of the sentence.", "Kael Veyrin laughed.", "It was the best laugh in the world. It filled the room. It was rich and astonished and entirely sincere, and it made the clocks rattle on the walls. “The catch!” he said. “Oh, you are a find. Nobody asks. Do you know that? In four thousand years, not one in ten has had the sense. The catch, Arlo, is—” He leaned forward. His voice dropped. “There isn’t one. You say a single word, and I tell you everything. No price. No trick. I swear it on anything you like.” He smiled. “All I ask is that you say the word.”", "“What word?”", "“Yes.”", "Arlo looked at him.", "He looked for a long time. It was the longest he had ever looked at anything. He saw the pale amused eyes, the neat coat, the white shape of a smile that did not move. He thought of a drawer in the back of his head. And a very small, very stubborn, very sensible voice, which had grown up in a house where nobody ever gave anything away for free, said, quite calmly: If he’d really wanted you to know, he’d have just told you.", "“No,” said Arlo.", "He heard how thin his voice was. He did not let it stop him.", "“No, thank you. If you really knew, and you were really nice, you’d just tell me. You wouldn’t make me say anything first.” He drew a breath. It shook. “And you’re not nice. You’re being nice. It’s different.”", "There was a pause.", "In the steam-white face, something happened. It was not anger. Arlo would think about that, later, for a very long time. It was something much worse. It was a kind of attention, as cool and bright and sudden as the opening of a door onto a winter night, and for a moment the smile did not look like a smile at all.", "“Well,” said Kael Veyrin softly. “That’s new.”", "He straightened. He brushed an invisible speck from his lapel.", "“Pity,” he said. “It would have been so much easier.” And then, brightly, as one turns to the next item at a meeting: “Odette. Shall we talk about you?”"], "nx": "c3s3"}, "c3s3": {"id": "c3s3", "c": 3, "title": "What Odette Wren Did", "p": ["“I’LL GIVE YOU the truth, Arlo,” said Nana Wren, very fast. “I’ll give it to you for nothing. Listen to me, and don’t look at him, and for pity’s sake don’t take your eyes off me. You come from a long line of—”", "“Odette.”", "The word was a hand across a mouth. It was not even loud. It was only said, quite gently, in a voice like velvet, and all twenty-three clocks on the wall began, together, to tick.", "“—a long line of people who—”", "“Ah-ah. Spoilers.”", "The ticking got louder. It was not forward ticking. Arlo could feel that. It was a sound like a very large engine going into reverse, a deep, wet, grinding sound that came up through the floor into his teeth, and every shadow in the room swung round, as one, to the small figure in the cardigan. The golden Sunday light in the middle of the room went out like a snuffed match.", "“Nana!” screamed June.", "“I’m all right, sweetheart.” She was not. Her face was gray. Arlo saw her sway, and put out a hand to the back of the chair, and the knuckles went white. “It’s only a, it’s the old trick, the, he’s winding me down. It’s all right. It’s how he does it. He doesn’t touch you. He only reminds the clocks.” She laughed, a short breathless sound, and looked at him, and it was her eyes that Arlo would always see. They were not frightened. They were bright and fierce and entirely, magnificently furious. “It’s an awfully rude way to go. I’d hoped for a bit more fuss.”", "“Stop it,” said June. “Stop it. You stop it right now, you, you—”", "She flung up both hands. The sun on the ceiling roared. A blast of white fire went across the room, so bright that Arlo threw an arm over his eyes, and he heard the sound of the china bowl shattering, and a long hissing screech like a kettle left on a stove. Something cold and sharp and thin passed his ear. When he looked again there was a wide black scorch across the table, and a hole in the plaster of the wall as big as a dinner plate, and the figure of Kael Veyrin had been torn in half.", "It healed at once. It simply poured itself back together, like smoke closing round a stone. “That was very good,” it said, with sincere admiration. “Truly. You’ve got a great deal of power, little one. I shouldn’t waste it on me. I’m not terribly flammable.”", "“Where’s my mother?” said June.", "She was shaking so hard that her braid trembled. Her light-gray eyes had gone huge. She said it in a voice that was not the voice of an eight-year-old girl. It was flat and low, and it had a stone in the middle of it. “Where’s my mom? You know. You’ve been keeping her. I can tell. You know something about her, you’ve got that look—”", "“Claire?” said Kael Veyrin, with pleasant surprise.", "June went rigid.", "“Dear Claire,” he said. “Such talent. Such a pity about the, ah, circumstances.” He sighed. “No, no. I haven’t got her. I don’t know where she is. I wish I did. But I know who does, and I’d be perfectly happy to—”", "“Don’t you say her name,” whispered Nana Wren.", "“It’s a lovely name.”", "“Don’t.”", "“You’re quite right,” said Kael. “It’s an imposition. Only—” He looked about, and the smile, which had hardly changed since the moment he appeared, widened by one tiny, perfect degree. “It does tend to take people’s minds off other things.”", "And Arlo understood.", "It happened all at once. It was like seeing the second picture in one of those puzzles, the old woman who is also a young one. The talk about the mother, the soft voice, the gentle jokes about the jars and the cat: all of it had been a conjuror’s patter. It had been to keep every one of them looking one way. And while they looked, the clocks on the wall had gone on ticking backward, one tick at a time, and the old woman in the green cardigan had gone on getting smaller.", "“Nana,” he said.", "She was sitting down.", "She had sat in the wingback chair without anyone seeing. She looked very small in it. Her white hair had flattened. The spectacles with the violet lens and the amber lens had slipped down her nose, and she was holding them there with one finger, and she was smiling at him with a strange, bright, drowsy fondness, as people do when they have been up all night and the morning has finally come.", "“Come here, sweet pea,” she said. “Quickly. Both of you.”", "They ran.", "He did not remember crossing the room. He was on his knees on the rug beside the chair, with June on the other side, and the old woman’s hands came down on their heads, one on each. They were very light. They were warm. They smelled of lavender and wool.", "“I haven’t much time,” said Nana Wren, in a pleasant, ordinary, conversational voice. “So I shall be extremely rude and say it all at once. June. You’ll listen.”", "“I’m listening,” sobbed June.", "“Good girl. First. You’re to go out the back, down the stairs by the kitchen. Don’t use the front. He’ll have his people at the front within the quarter-hour, if he hasn’t already.” She coughed. It was a wet, tiny, awful sound. “Hollis Wood. You know it. Behind the old cannery, up the hill. You’ve been in it with me a hundred times. There’s an ash tree that was struck by lightning. It’s got a split down the middle like a door.”", "“The Ash Gate.”", "“The Ash Gate. It’s not on any map. It’s the way in. It’s—” She smiled. “It’s the way home, for people like us. Take the key. It’s in my left pocket.”", "June’s hand went into the green cardigan. It came out with a small brass acorn, scratched and dull, on a loop of faded blue string. It was the shape of the knocker on the street door. It was warm.", "“You hold it up to the split. You say the words I taught you. You don’t stop, and you don’t look back. You don’t let anyone give you anything. Do you understand? Nothing. Not a sandwich. Not a hand. Not a hello.” She laughed again, very softly. “And you take him with you. You’re to look after him. And he’s to look after you. It’s going to be extremely difficult. You’ll be wonderful.”", "“I can’t,” whispered June. “I can’t, Nana, I’m not good at, I’m only level two—”", "“You’re the best witch in this street,” said Nana Wren. “I said so, and I’m never wrong.” She turned her head, slowly. “Arlo.”", "“I’m here,” said Arlo.", "“I know.” The amber lens flashed. “I should like to tell you who you are. It’s the one thing in the world I wanted to do. But I’m afraid I’m not going to have time, and I’m afraid there’s a gentleman here who would rather I didn’t.” Her eyes, brown and bright and ordinary, held his. “So I’m going to tell you something else. It’s smaller. It’s not nearly as grand. But it’s the only thing I’m sure of.”", "“What?”", "“You said no,” said Nana Wren. “You said no to the one thing in the world you wanted most. I’ve known witches of a thousand years who couldn’t have done it. You’re eight years old, and you’ve got a better heart than the whole of the Council put together.” Her small hand tightened on his hair. “Don’t you ever let anyone tell you that’s the same as being small.”", "“Nana,” said Arlo. He was crying. He had not noticed. “Nana, please. Don’t.”", "“Oh, I wouldn’t, if I could help it.” She smiled. “Now. The leaf, dear.”", "He fumbled it out. The pencil tin was in his pocket. Bartholomew was curled right down at the bottom, flat and small and trembling, as if he knew. Nana Wren took the tin in one palm. She breathed on it. It was only a breath. And the little leaf lifted its head, and wound itself, with great tenderness, around her little finger, and held on.", "“You’ll keep him safe,” she said.", "“I will.”", "“He’s a good one. He knows the way.” She let go. “Humphrey.”", "The cat was there. He had come down off the bookcase without a sound. He sat on the arm of the wingback with his tail wrapped round his feet, and for once he did not look in the least superior. He looked very old, and very small, and extremely sad.", "“Odette,” he said.", "“You’re to hold the door.”", "“I am aware.”", "“Don’t be a hero. You’re bad at it.”", "“I have been a hero,” said Humphrey, in a low, quiet, shaking voice, “on seven occasions, and I will have you know that I was excellent.” He pressed his orange head against her hand. “It has been an honor.”", "“It’s been dreadful,” said Nana Wren, with great affection. “I’ve had a lovely time.”", "The clocks stopped ticking.", "It was the silence that Arlo noticed. All twenty-three of them, all at once, in a long, soft, final hush, as the sea sounds when it goes out. And Kael Veyrin, who had stood quite still the whole of this time, with his hands clasped before him and his head a little bowed, as a man does at a funeral, drew a long breath.", "“I do apologize,” he said, quietly. And, to Arlo’s horror, he meant it. It was in his voice. “Truly, Odette. You were the best hostess I’ve had in four hundred years.”", "“Go to the devil,” said Nana Wren.", "“I’ve been.”", "She laughed. It was the last thing she did. It was a small, delighted, wheezing laugh, the laugh of a woman who has just heard the end of a very good joke. The light went out of the amber lens, and the violet, and her hands lay open on the children’s heads, light as two leaves.", "And the blue flame in the corner, which had burned so patiently since the beginning, bowed, and went out."], "nx": "c3s4"}, "c3s4": {"id": "c3s4", "c": 3, "title": "The Back Stairs", "p": ["“Run,” said Kael Veyrin, almost gently. “Do. I’ll give you a head start. It’s only sporting.”", "They ran.", "He remembered nothing of the next ten seconds. He remembered afterward only that June had hold of his wrist, that she had gone from the floor to her feet and across the room in a single movement, and that her face, in the white glare of the sun on the ceiling, was the face of someone who has been struck and has not yet felt it. She dragged him through the kitchen. She flung open a door he had not known was there. She was crying, in huge ugly gasping sobs that she did not seem to know she was making, and she did not stop for a moment, and she did not let go.", "Behind them, the room exploded.", "It was Humphrey. Arlo would never forget it. It was a small, furious, orange, spitting, shrieking, unstoppable thing, and it launched itself across the flat at a tall white man made of steam with a noise like a tornado in a pet shop. A shelf came down. A jar broke. There was a sudden rush of sound, a wind that smelled of spice and snow, and a long roar that went through the walls and out into the street. And over it, clear and warm and utterly unruffled, a voice said, “Oh, do be sensible.”", "“Don’t look,” said June.", "The back stairs were steep and narrow and smelled of old frying oil. They went down in the dark, round and round, in a long clattering spiral, and they came out at the bottom into the kitchen of the seafood shack. It was black. It was cold. The great old fryers stood in rows under their sheets like a congregation of ghosts. And at the end of the room, past the counter, a back door with a bar across it stood open an inch to a smell of wet grass, and the night.", "June stopped.", "It was so sudden that Arlo ran into her. She had gone rigid, her hand still locked around his wrist, her face turned to the stairs. From high above, very faintly, through two floors, there came a tiny, final, heartbreaking sound. It was a cat’s voice, raised in a long, defiant, magnificent yowl.", "Then it stopped.", "“Humphrey,” whispered June.", "“June—”", "“He’s, they’re, Nana’s—” Her voice cracked right across the middle. “She’s dead. She’s sitting in her chair, and she’s, and I just left her. I just left her there. I didn’t even— I didn’t say—”", "“She told you to go.”", "“I know she told me!” She whirled on him. She was shaking all over. Her face was wet and red and fierce, and for one terrible moment she looked at him as though he were the cause of everything. “If you hadn’t had a dream! If you hadn’t, if I hadn’t looked in that stupid notebook, if I hadn’t been so, so pleased with myself, if, if I’d just left you alone like I was supposed to—”", "“I’m sorry,” said Arlo.", "“Stop saying that!”", "“I’m—” He stopped.", "It was a long moment. In the dark kitchen, with the sheeted fryers all around them and the smell of the night coming in at the door, the two of them stood and looked at one another, and neither of them seemed able to breathe.", "He had said it his whole life. He had said it to cereal boxes, and tables, and people who stepped on his feet. He had said it so often that it no longer meant anything at all, and he saw, with sudden clarity, that June knew it. He saw that she had listened to him say it for two days. She had not minded. But she minded now. She minded, because it was the only thing in the world that he had ever been taught to do when someone he cared about was hurt, and it was no use to anyone.", "“No,” said Arlo. “I’m not. I mean, I am. But that’s not what I— I don’t think it’s my fault. I don’t think it’s yours. I think it’s his. I think he did it on purpose, and I think he wanted us to stand in a kitchen and blame each other.” He swallowed. “I think that’s what he’s good at.”", "June stared at him.", "“And I don’t want to run,” he said, and his voice trembled all the way. “I want to go back up and, and, I don’t know. Punch something. But she said. She said we have to go. She said you’re the best witch on the street. And I haven’t got anyone else.”", "It was a long, long silence.", "Then June Wren wiped her nose on her sleeve, carefully, with both hands, like a person trying to put away a sheet of paper.", "“You’re quite annoying,” she said, in a thick, small voice. “You know that? You’re just so, so annoying, when you’re right.”", "“I’m sorry,” said Arlo.", "“Arlo.”", "“I mean— no, I’m not. I’m glad.” He tried to smile, and it went wrong, and he stopped trying. “Is that better?”", "“A bit.” She took his hand. It was a small, hot, damp, scarred hand, with ink on the knuckles, and it was gripping his as if she were drowning. “Come on. Quickly. Before I stop being able to.”", "They went out through the back door, into the dark."], "nx": "c3s5"}, "c3s5": {"id": "c3s5", "c": 3, "title": "Hollis Wood", "p": ["It was a very ordinary night.", "That was the thing he would remember. Behind the seafood shack there was a yard with a dumpster and a cracked basketball hoop, and beyond that a chain-link fence with a hole cut in it, and beyond that, black against the starless sky, the long low shape of Hollis Wood. Somewhere a dog was barking. A television flickered in a window. In the lane at the front of the building, a long way off, a car door slammed and an engine started, and nobody, nobody at all, looked up.", "“Does anyone know?” he whispered. “Doesn’t anybody— there was a, there was a fight, there was— there must be—”", "“They won’t see it,” said June. She was running again. “It’s the lane. It’s very good at being a lane. It keeps the noise in.” Her voice cracked. “It’s the thing Nana was proudest of.”", "They went through the hole in the fence. They went across a field of wet grass, and a ditch, and a rusty rail that had once been the fence of a railway, and with every step the wood loomed higher and blacker against the sky, until it was a wall.", "At its edge, June stopped. She turned and looked back.", "Arlo looked too.", "The whole of Alder Lane was dark, but at the top of the lane, a long way off, in a window above a closed seafood shack, a light was burning. It was a clear, still, steady, golden light, the color of a Sunday afternoon. It burned in the dark like a small, patient lamp left in a window for someone who was late.", "“She’d have wanted it left on,” said June, quite steadily. And she turned her back on it.", "They entered the wood.", "The first thing he noticed was the smell. It was damp and mossy and green, and underneath it, very faintly, there was a smell like rain on hot stone. He knew it. He had smelled it every night for three years. It was in his pillow, and his sleeves, and a pencil stub, and it filled the dark with a thick sweet sadness, as if the whole forest had been waiting a very long time to be walked into.", "They did not talk. They went up a path he could barely see. The trees were tall, taller than any trees in the town, with trunks like the pillars of cathedrals, and they leaned in on every side, so that overhead there was nothing at all but a dark tangle and the faint gleam of a moon. His feet slid in the wet leaves. The cold went through his sweater and into his ribs. Somewhere, a very long way behind them, a bell began to toll, thin and strange, although there was no church in the town that had a bell.", "“Is that him?” said Arlo.", "“I don’t know.”", "“Is he following us?”", "“I don’t know, Arlo.” June did not slow down. “I only know that Nana said run. So we’re running.”", "They ran until their lungs burned. They ran until the path, which had begun as a good gravel track, narrowed to a trail, and then a deer-path, and then was nothing but a pale thread laid across the roots. The trees came closer. The dark thickened. It was so black that Arlo could not see his own feet, and he put out his hands in front of him like a blind boy and felt bark, and moss, and damp, and then something that was neither.", "It was warm.", "He stopped.", "“June.”", "“What? What is it?”", "“Look.”", "In his pocket, the pencil tin was glowing.", "It was a thin green light, no brighter than a firefly, that leaked out through the crack of the lid and lay on the leaves around his feet. As he watched, the lid lifted. A tendril rose. Bartholomew stood up in the dark, with his five small leaves spread wide, and he turned, slowly, all the way round, like a compass needle finding north.", "And he pointed.", "They looked where he was pointing. It was the thing at the end of a path, between two black trunks, where the moonlight did not reach. It was just a gap in the trees, an opening, a place where the tangle of the wood drew back.", "And in the gap, quite small and quite clear, there was a light.", "It was blue. It was a pale, cool, trembling blue, the color of deep water, and it lay at the end of the path like a pool, and it was pulsing very slowly, like breath.", "Arlo’s knees went weak.", "He knew it. He had drawn it forty-two times. He had seen it every night since he was five, at the end of a path that wandered between the roots of enormous trees, and he had thought it was a dream. He had thought it was a story that his head told when it was lonely. And here it was, waiting, in an ordinary wood, at the back of an ordinary town, as though it had never been anywhere else.", "“It’s real,” he whispered. “It’s, June. It’s all real. The forest. The path. It’s— I’ve been—”", "“I know,” said June.", "She did not sound surprised. She sounded as though she had known for a very long time. She had put her hand in the pocket of her coat, and he saw that she was holding the brass acorn, small and warm and dull, and that her fingers were shaking.", "“That’s not the Gate,” she said. “The Gate’s further up. That’s something else.”", "“What?”", "“I don’t know,” said June Wren, in a very small voice. “I don’t know what it is. But I think it’s been waiting for you.”", "In the pale blue glow, the trees stood very still. A little wind came down the path, smelling of rain, and of stars, and moved the hair on Arlo’s forehead. It was not unkind. It felt, if anything, like a hand.", "And, quite clearly, somewhere in the heart of that blue, someone began to hum: a slow, tired tune that Arlo had never heard in his life, and had somehow always known."], "nx": "c4s1"}, "c4s1": {"id": "c4s1", "c": 4, "title": "The Ring of Six", "p": ["The hum came from the light, and the light came from a clearing, and the clearing should not have been there.", "Arlo knew it the way you know that a stair is missing in the dark. Hollis Wood was a small wood. He had seen it from the school bus window for a year: a ragged green smudge behind the cannery, no bigger than three football fields, full of broken bottles and bike trails. You could walk across it in twenty minutes. They had been walking for an hour.", "“June,” he whispered. “The wood’s too big.”", "“I know.”", "“We’ve gone further than the wood goes.”", "“I know, Arlo. I noticed about forty minutes ago and I decided not to say anything.” She was gripping the brass acorn so tightly that the string had left a red line across her palm. “Nana said the edges are thin around here. She said sometimes the Other Side leaks.” She swallowed. “I’d just like it on the record that I thought it would be a small leak.”", "The path ended at the edge of the light.", "It was not a big clearing. It was perhaps thirty feet across, perfectly round, with the trees standing back from it in a ring like people giving someone room. The grass was short and silver. And in the middle of the grass, set into the ground like a dropped coin, was a pool of water no wider than a kitchen table, glowing from below with a pale, cool, trembling blue.", "The humming came from the pool.", "“Don’t,” said June, as he took a step.", "“I’m not touching it.”", "“You’re walking toward it.”", "“I’m just looking.”", "“That’s how it starts,” said June, but she came with him.", "Around the edge of the water, standing on low stone plinths in a circle, were statues.", "There were six. They were about the size of real people, carved from a gray stone that had gone soft and green with moss, and they stood facing inward, toward the pool, with their right hands raised. That was all they had in common. One was a tall woman in a long coat, with a braid down her back. One was a heavy old man with a beard to his belt and the face of someone who had laughed a lot. One was a boy hardly older than Arlo, holding a lantern. One had a hood, and one had no head at all, only a neck and a sort of rough smooth stub, as if the sculptor had run out of patience. The last was a girl with her chin up. Each of them, on the palm of the raised hand, bore the same small carved symbol: a circle with a flame in it.", "And between the girl and the woman with the braid, the circle had a gap.", "It was a seventh plinth. It was smooth and clean and bare. It had been swept, Arlo thought; the moss had not been allowed to take it. There was nothing on it at all, except a small dent in the top, about the size of a pair of feet.", "“There were seven,” he said, and did not know why he said it.", "“There were seven of what?”", "“I don’t know.”", "He stood in front of the empty plinth for a long time. In his pocket, the pencil tin had begun to shiver. He took it out. Bartholomew stood straight up with his five leaves spread, and his whole small green body was pointing, with the hopeless longing of a dog at a window, at the bare stone.", "“Arlo,” said June, in an odd voice. “There’s writing.”", "It was cut around the rim of the plinths, in letters so old that the moss had filled them, and she knelt in the wet grass and scraped at them with her thumbnail. She was frowning in the way she did when she was trying to read a word upside down.", "“I can only do the easy ones,” she muttered. “Nana always said I rushed. Um. It says ‘stood.’ It says, ‘these, who stood—’ and there’s a bit, ‘between the’— world? No, ‘worlds.’ ‘Between the worlds and the dark.’ And then— hang on—” She sat back on her heels. “‘Remember them. For the next will not remember themself.’”", "“That’s not very cheerful.”", "“It isn’t meant to be.” She hesitated. “And at the bottom, there, on the empty one. It’s hardly there. I think it says ‘Not yet.’”", "“Not yet what?”", "“Just ‘not yet,’” said June.", "Arlo put out his hand.", "He had not decided to. It was the same way he had put out his hand in the library toward a leaf. His fingers went toward the bare plinth, and stopped an inch from it, and the stone, without a sound, began to glow.", "It was only a flicker, a thin blue shimmer racing along the top like the first light on water. But the other six plinths answered. One after another, around the ring, the carved symbols on the raised palms woke up, small and bright, a circle of six small flames. In the pool, the humming deepened into a note he felt in the soles of his shoes.", "Something opened in the water.", "It was not a face. It was bigger than a face. It was a great round eye, ringed in fire, the blue of the deepest part of the sea, and it lay at the bottom of the pool and looked at him. It was the size of a dinner plate and it was the size of the sky. It was very, very old. There was no cruelty in it. It was only looking, steadily and with enormous gentleness, in the way that a person looks at a house they lived in once, a long time ago, when they come back and find a light in the window.", "“Oh,” breathed Arlo.", "“What?” said June. “What is it? I can’t see anything, there’s just— it’s just a pool. Arlo? Your eyes have gone funny.”", "The eye closed.", "It did it slowly, like a person lowering a lid, and the hum died, and the blue went out of the grass. The six symbols faded one by one. The clearing was dark and wet and ordinary, with six moss-green statues and a puddle.", "Arlo found that he was sitting down. He did not remember sitting.", "“What did you see?” said June.", "“An eye.”", "“Whose?”", "“I don’t know.” He was shaking. His hand, the one he had stretched out, felt as if he had held it in front of a fire. “It knew me. It, June, I think it was glad.”", "June looked at him for a long moment. Then she looked at the empty plinth. And then she did a very sensible thing, which was to stand up, take him by the elbow, and say, in a voice as steady as she could make it: “That’s enough of that. Let’s get to the Gate.”", "“But—”", "“Now,” said June. “Before it tells us anything else. I’m not ready for it to be nice.”", "They had gone perhaps twenty steps back into the trees, when Arlo heard the first thing in the dark that was not an owl.", "It was a voice. It was a long way off, high and bright and cheerful, singing a nursery rhyme. It was a song that every kid at Harlow Road knew. Ring around the rosie.", "It was being sung by someone who was walking very slowly toward them, and who sounded, quite clearly, as if he was having a wonderful time."], "nx": "c4s2"}, "c4s2": {"id": "c4s2", "c": 4, "title": "The Boy with the Silver Ring", "p": ["“Hellooo,” said Dexter Crowe.", "He stood at the edge of the clearing in the blue-gray dark, and he was smiling.", "It was the same smile. Arlo had seen it a hundred times, the fixed, easy, pink-cheeked smile of a boy who had always known exactly where he stood. But there was something wrong with it. It was too steady. It had not moved at all while he spoke, and his eyes were open too wide, as though someone behind them were holding the lids up with both hands.", "He was alone. He had no flashlight. His school sweater was torn at the shoulder and covered in burrs. His shoes were soaked, and one lace was undone, and he did not seem to have noticed.", "“Dexter?” said Arlo.", "“Oh, there you are,” said Dexter Crowe, delighted. “I’ve been looking everywhere. Hasn’t the wood got big?”", "It was not his voice.", "It was Dexter’s mouth, and Dexter’s throat, and it was Dexter’s accent that was making the words, but the rhythm in them, the warm, amused, perfectly measured music, belonged to someone else. It was like watching a puppet with a very good puppeteer. Arlo’s stomach turned over.", "“Don’t,” said June, very low. Her hand closed on his arm. “Don’t say anything. Don’t look at him. It’s— he’s been—”", "“How very rude,” said Dexter, pleasantly. “Don’t mind her, dear boy. She’s upset. She’s had a bad night.” He tilted his head. The movement was loose and strange, as if the neck had been oiled. “I do hope you didn’t think I’d let you leave without saying goodbye.”", "“You’re not here,” said Arlo. His voice cracked. “You said you left most of yourself in the basement.”", "“And so I did! But, you see, I’ve found the most obliging little house.” Dexter held up his right hand.", "He wore a ring on every finger. He always had. But on his index finger, among all the gold and brass and plastic, there was a new one. It was plain and thin. It was the dull gleaming silver of a very old coin, and as Arlo looked at it, it seemed to pulse, very faintly, like something with a heartbeat.", "“It was a gift,” said Dexter, with an air of fond reminiscence. “A man at the fry truck, at the end of the lane, such a lovely man. He gave the three of them fries, on the house, and a little something extra for the one in the middle who looked so sad. Dexter wanted his father to come home, you know. It’s a perfectly natural thing to want.” He sighed. “And I said, why, that’s the easiest thing in the world. Only take this. Only keep it on. Just till we’ve had a little talk.”", "“Where are the twins?” said June.", "“Oh, the Fitches. They wouldn’t. Boys with sense, those two.” Dexter smiled. “They ran all the way home. I should keep an eye on them. They’re going to be very difficult.”", "A cold, tiny wind moved through the trees.", "“Let him go,” said Arlo.", "“Let him go?”", "“It’s Dexter. He’s eight. He’s a, he’s not— whatever you want, it’s us, you don’t need—” His voice shook. “Let him go home.”", "“Oh, but he’s so useful.” The smile did not waver. “Do you know I haven’t had a single boy so deeply in debt in a hundred years? They’re so rarely this lonely.” Dexter took a step. His shoelace dragged in the leaves. “And now, my dears. As you’ve been so very kind as to walk so far, I thought we might do this the civilized way. I won’t hurt you. I’ve explained. I can’t reach you, Arlo. It’s a very great nuisance. But the boy can. And the boy’s hands are perfectly good.”", "“What are you going to make him do?” whispered June.", "“Nothing at all. I only need him to bring you somewhere.” A pause. “Somewhere I can speak to the two of you. At greater length.”", "“No,” said Arlo.", "“It wasn’t a question, dear.”", "Dexter ran.", "It was horribly fast. One moment he was ten yards away, and the next he was a blur of torn sweater and flailing arms, with his mouth wide open in a grin that would never, ever have been his own. June screamed. She flung out her hand, and a bolt of white light cracked across the clearing, and the grass in front of him exploded, and he did not so much as slow down.", "“I can’t,” she gasped. “Arlo, I can’t, it’s a kid, it’s Dexter, I can’t hit him—”", "“Don’t!”", "He caught her wrist. He was not sure why. He only knew, in a flash, like seeing the whole of a stair at once, that there was something about the ring. He had looked at it. He had looked for exactly the length of time it takes to see a thing, and a very small voice at the back of his head, the hamster voice, the one that went hamsters like warm places, and dark ones, said: It’s warm.", "The ring was warm. It was the only warm thing on him. It was glowing a very faint, very sick silver, in a sweater that was soaked with mud.", "“It’s the ring!” he shouted. “June! It’s a gift, it’s the thing he took, it’s the thing that— if he takes it off—”", "“He won’t!”", "“Then I will!”", "He was running before he had decided to. It was a stupid, hopeless, ridiculous thing to do, and it was the fastest he had ever run in his life. Dexter hit him in the middle of the clearing like a bus. They went down together in the wet grass, rolling, with the pool sparkling on one side and the statues spinning overhead, and Dexter’s hands, big and strong and hot, closed around his neck.", "“There you are,” said the voice, very pleasantly, in his ear.", "Arlo could not breathe. He grabbed at the hand. It was slippery with mud. The silver ring pressed against his jaw, and it burned like cold iron, and he clawed at it with every finger he had, and it would not move. It was like trying to pull a tooth out of a cliff.", "“Bartholomew,” he gasped. “Bartholomew!”", "It was the stupidest thing he could have said. It was the only thing he could think of.", "The pencil tin hit the grass beside his head and burst open.", "There was a streak of green. It was so fast that he did not see it leave the tin. It was a thread of living light, a tendril as long as his arm, and it whipped through the air and wrapped itself, five times, around Dexter’s fist. The leaves flared wide. They lit up, one by one, with the clean pale green of the inside of a lime. And the ring, which would not move for anything in the world, gave a thin, high, furious shriek, like a kettle just before it boils.", "“No!” cried Dexter, in a voice that was a great deal more than a boy’s. “No, no, no, you impertinent little—”", "The ring cracked.", "It did not break. It simply came open, from one side, like a pea-pod. The silver went dull. A little wisp of black smoke came out and curled up toward the trees, and the whole of Dexter’s body, which had been as stiff as a coat-hanger, went slack at once, like a puppet with the strings cut.", "He fell on top of Arlo with a thump.", "For a moment, nobody moved.", "Then Arlo felt a hand scrabbling at his shirt. It was a small, scared, scraped hand. And a voice, thin and high and cracked and entirely, wonderfully, recognizably annoying, said:", "“Pruitt? What, where— why’s it so dark?”"], "nx": "c4s3"}, "c4s3": {"id": "c4s3", "c": 4, "title": "What Dexter Wanted", "p": ["They sat him down on one of the plinths.", "It was June’s idea. It was a bit of a joke, perhaps, that the boy who had spent three years being horrible to everyone at Harlow Road should end up sitting in a ring of statues in the dark, hugging his knees and shaking like a wet dog. But nobody laughed. He had been sick on the grass. His face was green. He had lost, somewhere, about eight inches of his normal height, and was a small, damp, shivering child with a split lip and an extremely bad haircut.", "“I don’t remember,” he kept saying. “I don’t remember anything. I was at, we were at the fry truck. There was a man. He said we could have some. I only wanted— and then I was—” He put his hands over his face. “I didn’t do anything. Did I do something?”", "“You tried to strangle me,” said Arlo, not unkindly.", "“I did not!”", "“A little bit.”", "“Oh.” Dexter’s face crumpled. “Oh, no.”", "June was standing over him with her arms folded. She was still pale, and her braid was coming out, and she had an air of heroic, trembling, enormously dignified fury, like a very small judge. “You were,” she said slowly, “possessed. By a, by a very old, very horrible man. Through a ring. A ring you took from a stranger.”", "“He said it was free.”", "“Nothing is free, Dexter!”", "“I know that!” Dexter wailed. He was crying now, openly, noisily, with great hiccupping sobs, the way a child cries when he is much too tired to be proud. “I knew! I’m not stupid! I knew it was a trick! But he said—” He gulped. “He said he’d bring my dad back.”", "It was extremely quiet in the clearing.", "“Your dad?” said Arlo.", "“He left. In the summer.” Dexter wiped his nose on his sleeve, a large, wet, shocking smear. “Mom said he’d gone to look for work. In Phoenix. But he doesn’t call. And she cries in the car when she thinks I can’t hear, and she stopped making the, she stopped making the pancakes on Sundays, and I—” He broke off. “And the man at the truck said, ‘I know a thing about that.’ He said he could fix it. And he gave me this—” He held up his hand, and the ruined ring lay in his palm like a dead beetle. “And all I had to do was say yes.”", "“And you said yes.”", "“Of course I said yes!” Dexter was shouting through the tears. “Wouldn’t you?”", "Nobody answered.", "It was a very long silence. Arlo was looking at the ground. He was thinking of a warm voice in a forest, and a soft hand held out in the dark. I know exactly who she is. It will break your heart. And I would be delighted to tell it. Simply for the asking.", "“No,” said Arlo, at last. “I said no.”", "Dexter stared at him.", "“It was the same thing. It was— he said he’d tell me who my mom was.” He heard his own voice. It was very small. “I wanted to. I wanted to so much that I didn’t even know how to say it. And he asked me to say yes, and I—” He swallowed. “I only said no because I’ve never gotten anything for free in my whole life. I didn’t trust it. That’s all. I wasn’t brave. I was just used to it.”", "“That’s not why,” said June, quietly.", "“It is.”", "“It’s a good reason,” said June. “Not a bad one. It’s the best reason.” She was looking at Dexter now, and the fury had gone out of her face, a little at a time, like water out of a basin. “Dexter. Nobody’s mad at you.”", "“I’m mad at me.”", "“Well, that’s allowed.”", "Dexter looked at his hands.", "“I was horrible to you,” he said to Arlo, after a long moment, in a very low voice. “Wasn’t I. The whole year. And you, you saved me, just now, you went and, and—”", "“It was the leaf.”", "“You jumped on me.”", "“I got jumped on,” said Arlo. “It’s different.”", "Something that was nearly a laugh shook out of Dexter, damp and cracked and horrified, and he put his head down on his knees. After a time, quite slowly, with the air of a boy lifting something much heavier than he had ever lifted, he said, into his sweater: “I’m sorry.”", "Arlo looked at him.", "He had spent a long time imagining this. He had imagined it in the dark, in a bed that was the size of a cupboard, on mornings when his shoulder hurt, and the speech he had made, in his head, was a very good one. It had a great many long words. It left nothing out. And now that it was here, it seemed to him that there was only one thing worth saying.", "“It’s okay,” said Arlo. “Not all of it. But some.”", "“Okay,” said Dexter thickly.", "“Not the stuff with the notebook.”", "“No. That was—”", "“That was awful.”", "“I know it was.”", "“Okay,” said Arlo, and surprised himself with a sigh that felt like setting down a very large bag. “Now go home.”", "“What?”", "“Go home. Now. Go straight home, and don’t go near the fry truck, and don’t take anything from anyone who isn’t your mom. Not a piece of gum.” He hesitated. “And if your mom stopped making pancakes, you could, I don’t know. Ask her. Tell her you miss them. She might want to hear it.”", "Dexter blinked. “That’s what you think?”", "“I don’t know anything,” said Arlo. “I’m eight.”", "“You’re weird,” said Dexter. But he got up. He stood for a long moment in the dark, small and wet and tousled, with his hands hanging at his sides. And then he held out the broken ring, with the air of a boy offering something enormously shameful, and Arlo, after a moment, took it.", "“Which way’s home?” said Dexter.", "“Follow the path back. Don’t stop.” June was looking at him with an extremely complicated expression. “It’ll let you out. It likes people who aren’t supposed to be here to leave.”", "“Right,” said Dexter. He took two steps. Then he turned.", "“Pruitt.”", "“Yeah?”", "“What’s your deal? Are you, like, a wizard or something?”", "Arlo looked at June. June looked at Arlo. It was a long and extremely eloquent look.", "“No,” said Arlo. “I’m just a guy with a leaf.”", "Dexter Crowe nodded slowly, as though this made a great deal of sense, and walked off into the trees. He did not look back. They heard him going, a long way, crashing through the undergrowth, until the sound became a rustle, and the rustle became a whisper, and then there was only the wood.", "“That,” said June, with a long shaky breath, “was the nicest thing I’ve ever seen anybody do to a bully.”", "“He was possessed.”", "“Not the part where he was possessed. The part after.” She pushed the loose hair off her forehead with the back of her wrist. “It’s annoying. I was going to be furious with him for years.”", "“I know. I’m sorry.”", "“Arlo.”", "“It’s a habit,” said Arlo, and she laughed, a short, startled, wet little bark. And in his hand the broken silver ring went suddenly cold, and crumbled into a small pile of gray ash, which blew away on the wind."], "nx": "c4s4"}, "c4s4": {"id": "c4s4", "c": 4, "title": "The Ash Gate", "p": ["The ash tree had been struck by lightning a long time ago.", "Arlo had expected it to be dead. It wasn’t. It stood alone on the crown of a low, grassy hill at the very top of the wood, huge and black against a sky that had begun, faintly, to pale in the east. It was wider than a house. Its great limbs reached up in a tangle of silver twigs and bright new leaves, and down its trunk, from the highest branch to the roots, ran a long, clean, black split, wide enough for a man to walk through, as if the tree had been opened, very gently, with a knife.", "Inside the split, there was nothing. It was bark and darkness and a smell of wet wood.", "“That’s it,” said June.", "She said it very softly. She had stopped at the foot of the hill, and now she stood in the grass, with her hands clasped in front of her, looking up. The wind lifted her braid. Her face in the first gray light was white and pinched and very young.", "“Nana brought me here when I was five,” she said. “She told me it was a secret. She said it was the back door to the biggest house in the world, and that it was my job to remember where it was. I said it was a tree.” She laughed. It went wrong in the middle. “She said, ‘Everything’s a tree, till it’s a door.’”", "“June.”", "“I’m all right.”", "“You don’t have to be.”", "“I know.” She wiped her eyes briskly with the back of her hand, and lifted her chin, in a small, fierce, exactly Nana Wren way. “But I’d rather be all right later. Come on. I need you to knock when I say.”", "“Why me?”", "“Because Nana said, ‘Take him with you.’ And I’ve decided that means you do the knocking.”", "They climbed the hill together.", "Close up, the tree was much, much bigger. The bark was deeply grooved and warm to the touch, like the flank of a sleeping horse. In the split, at about the height of Arlo’s head, he saw a small round hollow, perfectly smooth, the exact size and shape of an acorn.", "June held up the key.", "It was only a bit of brass on a loop of string, a little battered, a little bent. She put it into the hollow, and it fit as though it had been born there, and the whole tree gave a long, slow, creaking sigh, like a person settling into a favorite chair.", "“By root and ring,” said June, in a low, trembling voice, “by ash and flame. I knock in my grandmother’s name.”", "She turned to Arlo, and nodded.", "He raised his hand. His knuckles, he noticed, were scraped, and there was a smear of dirt across them, and he was suddenly, painfully aware that he had never knocked on a door in his life that anyone had been glad to open. He did it anyway. He rapped on the black bark, three times, and then two.", "The wood went silent.", "Then, from somewhere deep inside the trunk, from the roots, from the heart of the tree, a voice spoke. It was not loud. It was dry and slow and old, like the creak of a heavy branch in a wind, and it said, quite distinctly:", "“Who knocks?”", "June drew a breath. “June Wren. Daughter of Claire Wren. Granddaughter of Odette.”", "There was a pause.", "“Odette Wren,” said the tree, “is dead.”", "It was not cruel. It was only a thing the tree knew, laid down, plainly, like a stone. It was said in the way that old things say the facts they have kept: without hurry, without apology. June’s face went blank. For one dreadful moment she did not move at all, and Arlo, watching, saw her hand tighten on the string until her knuckles turned white.", "“Yes,” said June, in a very small, very clear voice. “I know. That’s why I’m knocking.”", "The tree said nothing for a long time.", "“Then you knock,” it said at last, a little more gently, “in a good name. I am sorry for it. She was a friend of mine. She used to bring me tea and complain about her knees.” A pause, like the settling of a very old board. “Who is the one beside you?”", "“He’s with me.”", "“That is not an answer.”", "“He’s my friend,” said June fiercely.", "“Is that all?”", "June opened her mouth. Nothing came out. She looked at Arlo, very quickly, with a wide, frightened, desperate look, and he understood that she did not know, and that she did not dare to guess.", "He stepped forward.", "“I’m Arlo,” he said.", "His voice shook. He had been told, tonight, by three separate people, not to give anything away. But it was his own name, and he did not know what else to say, and it seemed to him, in some strange, deep, steady part of himself, that you did not lie to a tree.", "“I’m Arlo Pruitt. I’m eight. I live on Thornbury Court with some people who don’t like me very much. I don’t know why I’m here, or what I am, or what a, a Veyr— I don’t know anything.” He swallowed. “I only know a man in my dreams wants me to say yes to something, and I won’t, and everyone I know keeps getting hurt, and Nana Wren said we should come here. And I think she was right.”", "It was the longest speech he had ever made in his life. When it was over, he found that his hands were trembling and his face was wet.", "The ash tree rustled.", "“An honest answer,” it said, slowly. “It is the rarest sort. Most who stand where you stand have a great deal to say about what they are. You have said only what you are not.” The great limbs stirred overhead, and a shower of cold dew came pattering down on their heads. “The rest will come.”", "“Does that mean—” said June.", "“It means,” said the tree, “that it has been three hundred years since I opened this door for one who did not already think he knew what was behind it. Come in, child. Come in, little one. Mind your heads.”", "And the split in the trunk, which had been black, was suddenly full of light.", "It was a soft, rich, tender gold, the color of sun through a window. It poured out of the tree and lay on the wet grass in a long path, and in the midst of it, quite distinctly, they could see the beginnings of a stair. It went down into the ash, and down, and down, and its steps were made of roots as thick as pillars, worn smooth by a great many feet.", "June took Arlo’s hand.", "“Ready?”", "“No.”", "“Me neither,” said June Wren. “Let’s go.”"], "nx": "c4s5"}, "c4s5": {"id": "c4s5", "c": 4, "title": "The Other Side", "p": ["They came out into the morning.", "It was not the morning they had left. It was brighter. It was colder. It had a sweet, sharp, ringing quality, as if every blade of grass and every drop of dew had been scrubbed. They were standing on a wide, flat ledge of stone at the top of a stair, with the ash tree a gnarled and faded memory at their backs, and before them the world fell away.", "He did not breathe.", "It was a valley. It was so vast that it took his eyes a moment to understand it. It lay between mountains the color of old silver, and it was green, and gold, and full of mist, and over the whole of it, in a high, clear, pearl-colored sky, floated a hundred islands.", "They were not clouds. He saw that at once. They were hills, great rough lumps of earth and rock, with grass on top and roots hanging down, and each of them carried a town. Spires. Bridges, thin and bright as spider thread, strung from island to island. Waterfalls poured off the edges and vanished in the mist below. He saw a golden dome. He saw a tower so tall that it seemed to scrape the underside of the sun. He saw boats, actual boats, drifting through the air with their sails full of wind, and a great pale bird the size of a whale turning slowly in the distance, with a village on its back.", "And over it all, in the east, huge and calm and low, stood two moons, a white one and a blue one, hanging side by side in the morning like a pair of tired eyes.", "“June,” said Arlo, very quietly.", "“Yes.”", "“We’re not in Ohio.”", "“No.”", "“It’s— there’s a whale.”", "“It’s not a whale,” said June, in a faint voice. “It’s a skyhaven. It’s a, they’re quite common. It’s a sort of bird. They’re very polite.” She was clutching his hand so hard that his fingers hurt. She was staring at the valley with her mouth open, and tears were running unnoticed down both her cheeks. “I haven’t been home in three years. I forgot how big it was.”", "“It’s real,” said Arlo. It was the only thing he could think of. “All of it. It was real all along.”", "“Yes.”", "“And you’ve been, you were just there? At school? Next to the, the, it was right there.”", "“We’re not supposed to tell,” said June. She sniffed. “It’s a rule.”", "He began to laugh. He could not help it. It started somewhere in his chest, a thin, wheezing, hopeless sound, and it grew, and grew, until he was bent over with his hands on his knees and the tears were pouring down his face and he could not have said whether he was laughing or crying. All his life, he had lived in a room the size of a closet. And the whole time, on the other side of a tree, there had been this.", "“It’s so big,” he said. “It’s so, it was so big.”", "“I know,” said June, and she laughed too, in a high, cracked, helpless way, and they stood on the stone ledge at the top of the world with their arms around each other, laughing and crying, in the first light of a morning that belonged to neither of them.", "It was a long time before either of them noticed the bell.", "It was a very deep one. It began somewhere far below them, in the heart of the valley, a single slow, ringing, enormous note, and the sound rolled across the mist like a stone dropped into a pond. Then a second bell took it up, higher and clearer, from one of the floating towers. Then a third. Then a dozen, from every island, from every spire, from every bridge, ringing out over the whole green valley until the air itself shook.", "June went white.", "“That’s the Gate-bell,” she whispered.", "“What does it mean?”", "“It means someone’s opened the Ash Gate. From the outside.” She turned to him, and her eyes were enormous. “Arlo, nobody’s done that in— Nana said it was forty years. Nobody’s come in from the human world since before I was born.”", "“Is that bad?”", "“I don’t know.”", "On the far side of the valley, a long way off, something was rising out of the mist. It was a flock of tiny dark shapes, rising in formation, wheeling in the air with a glint of silver. They were coming toward the ledge. They were coming very fast.", "“June,” said Arlo.", "“I see them.”", "“Are they friends?”", "“I don’t know that either,” said June Wren, with a great deal of dignity and a very small voice. “I’m only level two.”", "The first of them was close enough to see. It was a rider on a great gray bird like a hawk, with a silver cloak streaming out behind, and a spear, and a banner on the end of the spear in the color of the deepest part of the sky. And above the rider, as she swept down toward them over the edge of the stone, there was a voice, clear and strong and ringing, calling a single word across the sky.", "“Halt!”", "Arlo’s hand tightened on June’s. In his pocket, the little green tendril of Bartholomew curled round his thumb, quite calm, as though it had been expecting this all along. And behind them, down in the roots of the ash, a very old, very patient voice said, very softly, to no one:", "“Welcome, little one. We kept your place.”"], "nx": "c5s1"}, "c5s1": {"id": "c5s1", "c": 5, "title": "Halt", "p": ["The bird landed with a sound like a janitor shaking out a wet rug.", "Arlo flinched. He had not meant to, but his whole body had been waiting to be hit by something since about two in the morning, and it did what it had been practicing. Wind slapped his face. Grit stung his eyes. When he could see again, there was a gray hawk the size of a pony standing on the ledge in front of him, folding its wings one feather at a time, and it was looking at him with one flat gold eye as if he were a parking ticket.", "It smelled of dust and warm pennies.", "“Hands,” said the woman on its back. “Where I can see them. Both of you. All four.”", "She did not shout. She said it the way a crossing guard says stop, and Arlo’s hands were up before he had decided anything. Beside him, June’s were up too, though she had made a small furious noise first.", "The rider swung down. She was tall and thin and not young, in a gray cloak the color of the bird, with a spear in one hand and a silver glove on the other. Her hair was cropped short and had gone the white of old toothpaste. Something was moving in her cheek. She was chewing, slowly, with the grim patience of a woman who had been chewing the same thing since Tuesday.", "“Captain,” said a second voice, from above. “Captain, they’re kids.”", "“Kids have hands, Lieutenant.”", "“Yes, but—”", "“A kid opened the Ash Gate. I would like to know how a kid opened the Ash Gate, and I would like to know it before I’m made to explain it to seven people who take their tea very seriously.” The captain’s eyes went over them, once, and stayed on nothing. “Names. You first.” She pointed the spear, politely, at June.", "“June Wren,” said June.", "She said it in a small clipped voice, with her chin up. Her braid had come half undone, and there was a leaf in it. Arlo, who had spent two days learning her expressions, saw that she was holding herself very straight in the way you hold a glass that is too full.", "Something happened in the captain’s face. It was brief. It was the face of a person who has opened a drawer and found something she had forgotten she owned.", "“Wren,” she said. “Odette Wren’s?”", "“Granddaughter.”", "“Where is Odette?”", "Nobody said anything. June’s mouth opened and closed. A small wind came up over the ledge and moved the grass, and below them, in the valley, the bells went on and on.", "“She’s dead,” said Arlo.", "He had not planned to. It was only that someone had to, and June could not, and the word was lying on the ground between them like something dropped. “A man killed her. Last night. In her kitchen. She told us to come here.”", "The captain took the thing out of her cheek.", "It was a clove. A single dark brown clove, with a tiny wet head. She looked at it for a moment, turning it between her gloved finger and thumb, as if it might have an opinion. Then she put it back in her mouth, and her face smoothed over like a bed being made.", "“I see,” she said. “I am sorry, Miss Wren. She was a hard woman to impress and I was fond of her.” She turned her head. “And you?”", "“Arlo.”", "“Arlo what?”", "“Pruitt.”", "“Never heard it.” A pause. “Kindled?”", "“I don’t know what that means.”", "“No,” said the captain slowly. She looked at him, and it was a different look, longer, from the top of his hair to his shoes. His sneakers were the kind from the discount store, with a strip of tape on one toe. “No, I don’t suppose you do.”", "She lowered the spear.", "“Isaura Vance. Captain of the Wardens of the Gate. That’s Lieutenant Pike, who’s going to stop waving in a moment. The bird’s Mortimer. He doesn’t like strangers, so don’t take it personally when he does what he’s about to do.” Mortimer leaned forward and delivered a long, deliberate, wet exhale into Arlo’s ear. “You’re both coming with me,” said Captain Vance. “Not as prisoners. As, let’s say, guests with paperwork.”", "“Am I in trouble?” said Arlo.", "“I haven’t decided yet.”", "“Okay.” He thought about it. “Can I tell you what I’d like to know first?”", "“By all means.”", "“What’s a Warden?”", "The captain’s mouth did something that was not quite a smile and was related to one. “Mostly,” she said, “it’s a person who stands next to a tree for a very long time and hopes nothing happens.” She jerked her head at the gnarled gray bulk of the ash behind them. “Forty years. I’d say I’ve earned the surprise.”"], "nx": "c5s2"}, "c5s2": {"id": "c5s2", "c": 5, "title": "Mortimer", "p": ["He rode in front of the captain, with her silver glove clamped in the back of his sweater, and for the first five minutes he kept his eyes shut.", "It was not courage. It was arithmetic. He had worked out that if he did not see the drop, the drop would not know he was there. Wind took his hair straight back and held it. The bird’s wings beat slowly and heavily, a thump and a pause, a thump and a pause, like someone walking upstairs in a very tall house. Somewhere above him, Lieutenant Pike was talking to his own bird, a mottled, nervous creature called Dumpling, in the voice of a man trying to coax a cat down from a tree.", "“You’re doing great, Dumpling. You’re so brave. You’re a brave girl. Don’t look down. Oh, wow. Okay, that was a cloud.”", "“Open your eyes,” said Captain Vance, in his ear.", "“I’m fine.”", "“You’re clutching a bird.”", "“I’m not clutching. I’m holding on firmly.”", "“Mortimer’s a very old bird. If you pull out his feathers, he’ll remember it for a decade.” A pause. “Open your eyes. You’ll want to have seen it.”", "He opened one.", "The first thing he saw was a laundry line. It ran from a stone chimney to a pole, over a roof, and it was hung with gray socks and a child’s red coat. It was on a hill. The hill was floating. He knew it was floating because he could see the bottom of it, a fat, ragged, brown-gold underside, trailing roots like the legs of an upside-down spider, and below the roots there was nothing at all, for what looked like a mile.", "He opened the other eye.", "The islands were not drifting. That was the thing he had gotten wrong. From the ledge, they had looked like clouds that had picked up some dirt, loose and wandering, but up close each one was held in place by chains. Great black iron links as thick as a school bus ran down from every hill into the mist, and between the islands, rope bridges swayed, with planks of a dozen different woods and wire twisted round the broken ones. Somebody’s grandfather had mended that bridge. Somebody’s grandfather had been in a hurry, and had used what he had.", "It was breakfast time. That was the odd part. In the middle of the biggest, wildest morning of his life, the city had gone ahead and had breakfast. A man in an apron stood on a balcony with a bucket and flung something white over the rail, and a hundred small birds dropped out of the air to meet it. A boy about his age, in a striped scarf, was lowering himself down a bridge cable on a pulley with a school bag on his back, one foot braced against the rail, whistling. Two old women on a landing were arguing over a cart of cabbages. And from a hundred chimneys, smoke went up in thin straight lines, and then, at the height of the tallest roof, all bent sideways at once, the way a dog’s ears go back.", "“Why does the smoke go that way?”", "“The Draft,” said Captain Vance. “It blows through the gap between the mountains. Highmere’s built on it. You’ll learn to tell the hour by the smell.”", "“What’s Highmere?”", "“That.”", "She pointed with the spear.", "It was ahead, where the islands thickened, and for a moment he could not tell where the city began and the sky stopped. There were so many hills. They were stacked and slung and chained together, hundreds of them, tier on tier, in a loose bright heap that climbed the air like a bunch of grapes. Streets ran between them on stone arches. Towers stuck up through the middle like pins in a cushion, and every tower wore a different color of roof: a copper one, gone green; a white one; a roof of blue tile, with a tiny flag. Over the very top of it all stood a single slender spire, pale as a candle.", "“The Hearthcourt,” said Lieutenant Pike, drifting up alongside on Dumpling. “That’s where the Seven sit. Don’t say anything to them about the bridges.”", "“What’s wrong with the bridges?”", "“Nothing,” said Pike, a little too fast. “They’re all fine.”", "“They’re all fine,” said Captain Vance, very dryly.", "As they came down toward the city, it began to notice them. A face appeared at a window. A window opened. A girl on a rooftop in a yellow raincoat looked up, waved at the Wardens’ birds, and then, as they passed overhead, saw Arlo. Her arm stopped in mid-air. She stared, with her mouth a little open, at his sweater. At his jeans. At the toe of his left sneaker, which was held together with a strip of silver tape.", "He had been looked at his entire life, and he knew a hundred kinds of stare. This one was new. It was not scorn, and it was not pity. It was the stare you give a pig that has walked into church.", "“Why is everyone looking at my shoes?” he said.", "“Because nobody’s worn those in a long time,” said the captain. “They haven’t got rubber here. Not like that.” A pause. “They’re not looking at your shoes, Arlo. They’re looking at the fact that I’m carrying a stranger.”", "“Is that bad?”", "“It’s very rare. It’s not a thing the city does.” The glove tightened a very little in his collar. “The last stranger was forty years ago, and he left.”", "“How?”", "“Not by choice.”", "Arlo did not ask any more questions. He shut his eyes again, quickly, and held on to the bird, and listened to the bells.", "There were so many of them now. They came from every tower, each at its own pitch, so that the whole sky was full of a clanging, shivering music, high and low, close and far, like a hundred spoons being dropped down a hundred stairs. Somewhere in it he could hear the bell of the first tower, the deepest, still going: dong, and a long wait, and dong. It rang the way the school fire alarm rang in a drill. Everyone was pretending it was a drill. Nobody was sure."], "nx": "c5s3"}, "c5s3": {"id": "c5s3", "c": 5, "title": "The Cake", "p": ["The wardhouse stood on the lowest island in the city, a hump of rough gray rock the locals called Underbridge, because every bridge in Highmere eventually ran over it.", "It did not look like a place that was frightened of anything. It looked like a place that had been left out in the weather. The building was low and square and made of the same stone as the island, with a slate roof, a flag, a boot-scraper by the door, and a very large brass bell that somebody had hung a hat on. There were barrels of rainwater in the yard. There was a bench with a cat on it. The cat was asleep. When the captain walked past, it opened one eye, shut it, and rolled over, with the exhausted authority of a creature who had seen every one of them come in and had not once been impressed.", "Inside, there was a room.", "It was a small plain one, with a table and four chairs, a window that looked out across a slice of sky, and a plate. The plate was in the middle of the table. On it was a loaf of dark cake, shiny on top, studded with something yellow and cut into thick, neat, trembling slices. The smell hit Arlo from the doorway. It was honey and butter and burnt sugar, and it went up his nose and straight to the part of his brain that had been awake for twenty hours and had eaten, in that time, half a turkey sandwich.", "“Sit,” said Captain Vance. “Someone will bring tea. Someone will bring the Registrar. Neither of them will be quick.” She paused at the door. “Eat what you like. It’s the canteen’s. Pike, you’re with them.”", "“Yes, Captain.”", "“Don’t talk.”", "“I never—”", "“Lieutenant.”", "The door closed.", "There was a pause, and then Pike dropped, with an enormous sigh, into the nearest chair, pulled off his helmet, which had a boiled egg in it, and began, quite cheerfully, to talk.", "“So,” he said. “That went better than I thought. I thought she was going to put you in the cell. We’ve got a cell. It’s very small, there’s a spider in it called Gerald. Is that a good cake? It’s a good cake. It’s Hobb’s. He’s the cook. Don’t have the soup.”", "“Okay,” said Arlo.", "“It’s fine, it’s just a bit—” Pike searched for it. “Murky.”", "Arlo did not move.", "June had already pulled a slice toward her, and was holding it in both hands, looking at it in a numb sort of way, like a person who has been handed a baby. But Arlo stayed where he was. He stood on the stone floor with his hands at his sides and looked at the cake, and the cake sat there, shining, perfectly placed, in the middle of the table in a room he did not know, in a place where he had been told by every person who loved him: Don’t let anyone give you anything.", "It was a very good cake. He thought that was the worst thing about it.", "“Is it free?” he said.", "Pike blinked. “Is it—”", "“Do I owe anyone for it?”", "“It’s cake.”", "“I know. I mean, does somebody want something, after? Because if somebody wants something I’d like to know before I, before.” His voice was getting thin. He cleared his throat. “I’d just like it to be, I’d like it to be on the, I don’t know. On the level.”", "There was a silence.", "Pike’s face went through three or four things. He was a young man, with an open freckled face and the sort of ears that stuck out, and it was clear that he had never in his life been asked such a question, and that he had a feeling, somewhere in the middle of him, that it was a bad one.", "“It’s the Wardens’ food,” he said, slowly. “It’s, um, billed to the city. It’s, you’re a guest. You’re allowed.” He scratched his neck. “Nobody’s going to want anything. It’s just cake.”", "“He’s had a night,” said June quietly.", "“I can see that.”", "“Somebody gave a boy a ring.”", "“Ah,” said Pike, though plainly he did not.", "Arlo pulled out the chair.", "He sat. He took the smallest piece, and he held it, and for a long moment he did not do anything with it at all. It was heavy. It was warm. It was sweet and dark and slightly sticky, and as he finally put it in his mouth, the honey and butter and burned sugar hit all at once and he could feel his eyes sting.", "He ate the whole slice in four bites without looking at anyone.", "“Slower,” said Pike gently. “It’ll still be here.”", "“Sorry.”", "“No. I mean, nobody’s going to take it.”", "It was the worst thing he could have said. Arlo’s throat closed. He put the plate down, quite carefully, on the table, and stared at it, until a hand slid something onto the plate. It was June. She had broken her own slice in half, and she was pushing the bigger piece toward him, with her eyes on the wall.", "“Your turn,” said Arlo, hoarsely.", "“What?”", "“You gave me half a sandwich.” He pushed it back. “Now it’s your turn.”", "June looked at him. She looked at the half slice of cake. Then, very slowly, with the face of a girl who has discovered that something is still funny, she began to laugh. It was weak and shaky and ugly and there were tears in it, and she put her forehead on the table among the crumbs, and her whole back shook. Arlo, not knowing what else to do, patted her on the shoulder, a stiff little tap-tap-tap, like a man testing a melon.", "“You’re terrible at that,” said June, into the table.", "“I know.”", "“Do it again.”", "He did it again.", "Lieutenant Pike took a very great interest in his egg."], "nx": "c5s4"}, "c5s4": {"id": "c5s4", "c": 5, "title": "Form 14-C", "p": ["Registrar hesper Quill arrived with four stamps, a lamp, and a look of enormous personal suffering.", "She was a very small, very upright woman with spectacles on a chain and ink on both thumbs, and she entered the room backward, because she was holding the door open with her hip and carrying a stack of paper so tall that it came up to her nose. She set it down on the table. It made a noise like a dropped building. A dragonfly made of brass, no longer than Arlo’s finger, was perched on her shoulder, and as she sat, it flew down and landed on the corner of the table and began, with great energy, to clean its face.", "“That’s Stamp,” she said, without looking up. “He’s very good, and he bites. Don’t touch him. Names.”", "“We gave them to the captain,” said June.", "“The captain is not a form.” Quill drew out a sheet, uncapped a pen, and glared at them both over her spectacles. “I will require names, dates, places of birth, sponsoring household, point of origin, mode of entry, purpose of visit, and whether either of you has, to your knowledge, been bitten, burned, cursed, or enchanted in the last seven years. In writing. Be tidy.”", "It took forty minutes.", "It would have taken longer, but June had spent her whole life in houses with paperwork, and answered every question in a flat, quick, grown-up voice, like someone who knew exactly what was wanted and would get it over with. Name: June Alder Wren. Born: Highmere, third island, Lantern Row. Sponsoring household: Wren, of the Pendle Stair. Arlo, listening, discovered that she had a middle name. He had not known. It seemed like the sort of thing he should have asked.", "Then it was his turn.", "“Date of birth?”", "“Um. October ninth.”", "“Year?”", "He told her. She counted on her fingers, nodded and wrote. “Place of birth?”", "“I don’t know. I was left at a hospital.”", "“What hospital?”", "“I don’t know that either.” A pause. “There was a blanket. And a note pinned to it. Not on the hospital, on the blanket.”", "“What did the note say?”", "“It only said Please.”", "Quill’s pen stopped.", "It was only a little hitch, the length of a breath, and then it began to move again, but Arlo, who had grown up watching adults to see which way the wind was going, saw her glance, very quickly, at Captain Vance, who had come in quietly and was leaning on the wall by the window with her arms folded. Neither of them said anything.", "“Sponsoring household?”", "“Um. The Pruitts? On Thornbury Court?”", "“I have no jurisdiction there. Who are they in relation to you?”", "“Foster. They’re, they’re sort of paid to have me.”", "“Ah,” said Quill, and wrote something short, and then, to his surprise, crossed it out and wrote something else. “Species of arrival. Box one: Kindled, born or sworn. Box two: Fey. Box three: Revenant. Box four: Other, specify.”", "“I don’t, can I be a four?”", "“You are a not-one.” She sniffed. “Hold out your hand.”", "He held it out. She took a small round object from her pocket, the size of a quarter, clear and flat as a window, and laid it in his palm.", "“Tell me if it warms,” she said. “It warms for anything with a seal. A spark, a thread. Even a very small one.”", "They waited.", "It was not warm. It lay in his hand like a piece of the sky from a very cold morning, and the longer he held it, the colder his palm felt. He saw June lean forward. He saw the dragonfly stop cleaning its face. He heard the stone, a faint dry hum, that seemed to come from the walls themselves.", "“Nothing,” said Quill. She took it back. She looked at it, for a long moment, with her mouth pursed, and held it up to the window as if she suspected a crack. “Well.” A pause. “That’s unusual.”", "“Is it wrong?”", "“It is a human child,” said Registrar Quill, “who has walked through the Ash Gate, which has not been opened in forty years, with no sponsor, no seal, no glow, and a leaf.” She paused. “It is the single least convenient arrival of my career. And I was here for the Ferret Incident.”", "“What do I put?” said Arlo. “For the box?”", "“Four.”", "“And, what do I write for ‘specify’?”", "She looked at him, over her spectacles, with an expression he could not read.", "“Whatever you like,” she said.", "He took the pen. He wrote it slowly, in his best handwriting, the one he used for the permission slips, and underlined it. It was the only thing he was certain of.", "Arlo.", "She took the form and read it. And for one moment, behind the spectacles, something in the grim small face changed. It was gone before he could name it.", "“Very good,” said Registrar Quill, rather gruffly. “Now. Miss Wren. We must discuss your grandmother.”"], "nx": "c5s5"}, "c5s5": {"id": "c5s5", "c": 5, "title": "The Name Nobody Says", "p": ["She asked it quite gently, and June answered it quite steadily, and neither of them seemed to notice that the room had gone still.", "She told it in order. It was the way she told everything: facts, arranged, one after another, like a child laying out cards. The library. The clipboard. The lane and the flat above the seafood shack, and the cat. The reading, and the bowl. She told them about the candle that burned blue. She told them how the twenty-three clocks had stopped on a single second. When she got to the part with the voice, she paused, and drew a long careful breath, and kept going.", "“And he said, ‘Do carry on, Odette.’ And Nana, Nana laughed. She said, ‘Go to the devil.’ And he said, ‘I’ve been.’” June folded her hands on the table. They were perfectly white. “Then she died.”", "The pen had stopped a long time ago. The dragonfly sat on the stack of paper, quite motionless, its brass wings folded.", "Quill took a handkerchief out of her sleeve. It was white, with a small blue border, and it smelled faintly of lavender and ink. She did not say anything. She pushed it across the table with one finger, the way you slide a coin across a counter, and looked away at the window while June used it.", "“The man who did this,” said Captain Vance, from the wall. Her voice was very level. “Did he give a name?”", "June looked at Arlo.", "“No,” she said. “But I know it.”", "“You know it.”", "“Nana said not to ask him for it. She said not to let him say it to us. But he did say it. In the library, Arlo told me. He said it to Arlo in the dream.” She swallowed. “It was Kael Veyrin.”", "The hum stopped.", "It took Arlo a moment to understand what had happened. There had been a low sound in the room since the moment he had walked in: a faint, even, dry buzz, like a refrigerator in the next apartment. It came from a row of small glass lamps set into the wall, each with a pale blue flame inside, and he had not known he could hear it until it was gone. The flames did not go out. They simply stood very still in their glass, like people who have heard something in the next room.", "“I beg your pardon,” said Registrar Quill, in a voice no louder than a pin.", "“Kael Veyrin,” said June.", "“Do not say that name in this building.”", "“It’s the one he said.”", "“It is a name from the Founders’ Book,” said Quill. She was gripping the edge of the table. Her knuckles were white. “It is a name from the oldest chapter of the oldest book we have. It is a, a story. It is a thing we tell the first-years on the first night, to frighten them into doing their homework.”", "“A fairy tale,” said Pike. He gave a little uncertain laugh. “I mean, he’s, that’s what he is, isn’t he? He’s the bogeyman. It’s the bit about the king, and the, with the gate, and the sealed—” He stopped. He looked at the three faces in front of him. “Captain?”", "“Nobody in this building,” said Isaura Vance, “says that name.”", "It was very quiet.", "“Why not?” said Arlo.", "He had not meant to. He had only been listening, with the cold slow creeping feeling of a boy at the edge of a pond in winter who hears the ice make a noise. “He’s real. I’ve talked to him. He’s been in my dreams since I was five. He knows my name.” His voice cracked. “He gave Dexter a ring. He killed Nana. I don’t see why it’s worse to say it than for him to do it.”", "“It’s not about worse,” said the captain.", "“Then what?”", "She looked at him.", "“It’s about a promise,” she said. “It’s a very old one. Somebody made it, a very long time ago, to a great many frightened people. They said, ‘We’ll keep the door shut.’ And the way you keep a door shut for a thousand years is, you stop talking about what’s behind it.” She pushed herself off the wall. “It’s not superstition, Arlo. It’s a habit. A very bad one, and I have a very good idea who it was made for.” She stopped. “But it has never, in my lifetime, been broken by a child in my hearing, and I find I have no idea what to do about it.”", "“Say, ‘thank you,’” offered Pike.", "“Lieutenant.”", "“Sorry.”", "Nobody moved. In the wall, the little blue flames stood quite still, and the dragonfly, with a small clicking whirr, took off from the stack of paper and landed carefully on the Registrar’s hair.", "“There’s one more thing,” said June, hesitantly. “Isn’t there, Arlo? Show them.”", "“Show them what?”", "“Bartholomew.”", "“Oh.”", "He took the tin out of his pocket. It had been in there so long that it had gone warm against his leg, and the lid, when he lifted it, came off with a small kiss of air. Bartholomew woke, stretched, and rose from the soil, five leaves wide, and looked around with perfect and unbothered interest. Pike made a sound like a kettle. The Registrar took her spectacles off.", "“Heartleaf,” she whispered.", "“It’s a leaf,” said Arlo. “It’s named Bartholomew.”", "“It is a heartleaf of the Tanglewood.” Quill had half risen from her chair. She was staring at the tiny green thing as if it were a snake, and as if the snake had asked her a very kind question. “It does not grow outside the Tanglewood. It will not take root in any soil in this world but the black loam under those trees. Nobody has carried one out of the Tanglewood in, Captain, it is ninety years. It has been shut for ninety years.”", "“Say that again,” said Captain Vance, very slowly.", "“Ninety years. The Quiet Century. The Hearthcourt voted. They closed it.”", "“Why?”", "“Because,” said Quill. She stopped. She seemed to hear what she was about to say. “Because of what was in it.”", "Arlo looked at the leaf. The leaf, perched on his finger, looked back. It tipped its head, in an unhurried and quite friendly sort of way, as though they had all been talking about the weather.", "“My dream,” said Arlo. “It’s a forest. It’s all in my notebook. A path, and trees that lean, and a blue light. It’s where he calls me from.” He swallowed. “June said that’s the Tanglewood.”", "There was a pause. It was about the length of a held breath, and it had a weight to it, and in the middle of it, he saw the Registrar’s face go the color of a plate.", "She did not say anything. She put on her spectacles. She picked up her stamps, one at a time, with great care, and put them in her pocket. She stood, and smoothed her skirt, and said in a thin high careful voice, to nobody in particular, “I shall be a moment,” and walked out of the room. The door closed behind her with a little click. The form, only half finished, lay on the table where she had left it, under the dragonfly.", "For a moment, nobody breathed.", "Then, from beyond the wall, from somewhere deep inside the building, there came a distant, muffled, rising babble of voices. It was the sound of a lot of grown-ups who had all just heard the same thing, and one word, pressed up out of the noise, went round and round: Tanglewood. Tanglewood. Tanglewood.", "Pike leaned over to his captain. He had lost his freckles somewhere in the last minute.", "“Is it true?” he whispered. “About, about the ninety years?”", "“Eat your egg, Lieutenant,” said Isaura Vance.", "But she did not take her eyes off the boy. She stood with her silver glove at her side, and the clove going slowly back and forth in her cheek, and she looked at him as you look at a door you have walked past every day for forty years and have just noticed has a handle.", "Outside, one by one, the bells of Highmere began to stop.", "The first to go was the deepest, the great bell of the lowest tower. Then the next. Then a dozen at once, the high bright ones and the cracked ones, until the whole sky, which had been clanging and shaking for an hour, was hushed and wide and listening. Arlo had seen a classroom do that, once, when the principal came in. It was the quiet of a great many people, all at the same moment, turning to look at the same thing.", "He found that he was holding June’s hand again. He did not know when that had happened.", "“Is that us?” he whispered.", "“Yes,” said June.", "“Is it bad?”", "“I don’t know,” said June, and her hand, in his, was cold as the glass in the Registrar’s palm and perfectly steady. “But I think now they’re going to want to meet you.”"], "nx": "c6s1"}, "c6s1": {"id": "c6s1", "c": 6, "title": "Meanwhile, in the Dark", "p": ["There was a drawing room at the bottom of the world, and it was the only comfortable place in all the seals.", "It had not been there at the start. At the start there had been nothing at all, which Kael Veyrin had found very dull, and so in the first century of his imprisonment he had built a room. It had a carpet, woven in a kingdom that no longer existed, in a red that nobody now had a name for. It had two armchairs, a fire that burned without fuel, a small table with a chessboard, and a brass trolley on which, every afternoon at four, a pot of tea materialized exactly as hot as he liked it. He did not know where the tea came from. He suspected the seal, which had a sense of humor, in the way a very old judge does.", "He sat in the better of the two armchairs with his legs crossed and a letter in his hand, and he was happy.", "“Pell,” he said. “I have lost a ring.”", "“Yes, sir.”", "“A perfectly good ring. Silver. I’d had it three days. And I lost it to a houseplant.”", "“A heartleaf, sir.”", "“Please don’t say it so fondly.”", "The other armchair held a very small person. He was perhaps the height of a doorstop, and he was made of gray moss and gray linen and a great deal of tired obligation, with a lantern for a head and a ring of enormous iron keys hung round his neck that clanked whenever he breathed. His name was Pell. He was the Turnkey. The gods had made him at the very beginning, from a last cupful of their patience, and set him to watch the prisoner. He had been watching ever since. Somewhere in the third millennium he had given up trying to look stern and had settled instead for looking like a man waiting for a bus that was running very late.", "“It was a very good ring,” said Kael, again, wistfully. “It had a boy attached. Eight years old, father gone, nobody at home to tell him no. It was beautiful. It was practically a gift to myself.” He sighed. “And then a leaf, Pell. A leaf. I’ve been bested by shrubbery.”", "“You did say, sir, in the second century, that you’d never be bested by anything you couldn’t eat.”", "“I did say that.” Kael looked at his teacup. “I’d like it noted that I haven’t tried.”", "The fire popped. Beyond the edges of the room, where the carpet stopped and the nothing began, something enormous turned over in its sleep, and the whole parlor trembled very slightly, and the teacup rattled in its saucer. Kael steadied it with one finger without looking. It was a very old habit. Four thousand years of earthquakes had given him the reflexes of a waiter.", "“Has Eternity written?” asked Pell, timidly.", "“Not yet.”", "“It’s been four thousand years, sir.”", "“She’s very busy. I send my regards every morning. It’s only polite.” He turned the letter over in his long fingers. “One doesn’t take offense. One simply keeps showing up. I find it wins them over in the end.”", "“Yes, sir.”", "“Do you know what they have never once done, Pell? In four thousand years?” He smiled. It was a lovely smile. It had been a lovely smile when the earth was young, and it had talked kings out of crowns. “They’ve never asked. They take what’s on the tray. Everyone does. A hundred thousand people, and a handful of kings, and all of them took the offer and not one asked what it cost.” He leaned back. “And then a boy of eight, in a woman’s kitchen, on his very first night of knowing I existed. What’s the catch?” He said it in a light high voice, with great delight. “I nearly fell off the chair.”", "“It was a good question, sir.”", "“It was an excellent question. It was the best question anyone’s asked me since a certain gentleman named Odalric asked how he could have been so stupid.” The smile did not go. But something in the room did. “I wasn’t going to have to answer him. I’d have had him on a plate by morning. I only needed one word. I was so close, Pell. And the little beast said no, thank you.”", "“Twice, sir.”", "“Say that again.”", "Pell opened his mouth, and shut it.", "“I didn’t quite hear,” said Kael Veyrin, in a voice that had no weight in it at all.", "The fire went out.", "It did not die. It simply stopped, as if somebody had pinched it. The room became cold, instantly and completely, and a thin white frost ran up the sides of the teapot and over the cup, and the steam rising from it froze in the air in a little delicate spiral and hung there. The pleasant, silly, long-limbed man in the armchair did not move. He sat perfectly, perfectly still, and the face that had been so ready to laugh had nothing in it at all. It was smooth and quiet and ancient. It was the face of a man who had been bored for four thousand years, and had just been shown the first thing in a long time that was not.", "“He said no to me,” said Kael, quite softly, “twice. The seventh. Of course it’s him.” He put the letter down on the arm of the chair. “I’m going to need him not to exist, Pell. It’s the only sensible thing. It’s a shame. I’d rather like to have had him to dinner.”", "Pell’s keys clanked, very quietly, as he breathed.", "Then Kael blinked, and the frost fell off the teapot in a little shower of sparks, and the fire leapt up as if it had never been away, and the parlor was warm and golden and absurd again. He smoothed his lapel. He gave the Turnkey a large, sunny, apologetic smile.", "“Forgive me,” he said. “One does so hate a scene.”", "“Of course, sir.”", "“Now. Business.” He unfolded the letter. “Has the post come?”", "Something flickered at the very edge of the carpet. It was a moth. It was the size of a man’s hand and black as ink, with wings of something that was neither silk nor smoke, and it had come through a crack in the dark that was thin as a hair, one of the very small cracks that had been opening in the seal for eight years. It fluttered across the room in great soft loops, and landed with exquisite manners on the arm of his chair.", "“There you are,” said Kael, fondly. “Good girl.”", "He had already written the letter. He had written it that morning, on paper made from a promise somebody had once broken, in a clear and elegant hand, and he read it over once more with his lips moving, as an author enjoys his own work.", "“‘Dear Lysander,’” he said aloud. “‘What a lovely week to be on the Hearthcourt. I do hope the memorial is moving. I’m told there’s a boy. Do take a look at him, and then do give him a small gift, for me. You’ll remember the favor you owe. I’ve kept it quite safe. Nothing too grand. A box will do. Yours, K.’”", "He folded it in three. He tied it with a thread of black light and put it into the moth’s patient legs.", "“Be nice,” he told it.", "The moth rose into the dark, and was gone.", "“Sir,” said Pell, in a very small voice. “If I may. You did say he can’t be reached. The boy. You said he’d refused, and a refusal is a wall.”", "“I did say that.” Kael steepled his fingers. “And a wall is a wonderful thing, Pell. It tells you precisely where not to knock.” He smiled, warmly, at the empty parlor. “I never said anything of the people standing next to him.”"], "nx": "c6s2"}, "c6s2": {"id": "c6s2", "c": 6, "title": "The House on Pendle Stair", "p": ["It was the crookedest house Arlo had ever seen, and it was alive.", "He was almost sure of it by the time they reached the front step. It stood at the very top of a flight of three hundred stone stairs, wedged between two much grander houses on two much grander islands, like a book somebody had shoved on a shelf and forgotten to straighten. It was nine stories high and about nine feet wide. It was painted a color that had once been yellow and was now the shade of old teeth. Every window was a different shape, and from every one of them, as they came up the steps, something bright and curious peered out and then dived out of sight.", "“It’s got a face,” whispered Arlo.", "“It hasn’t got a face.”", "“It’s got eyebrows.”", "“Those are shutters,” said June. “They’re expressive.” She was walking very fast. She had not said anything for three hundred stairs. Captain Vance had put them down at the foot of the long flight with an awkward, formal, clove-chewing pat on June’s shoulder, and a promise that someone would come for them at dusk, and had flown off, leaving them alone with a rope handrail and three hundred steps. Somewhere around the hundred-and-fiftieth, June had stopped being an eight-year-old giving orders and started being just a girl who was very tired and very frightened and a very long way from where she wanted to be.", "They reached the top. A brass bell hung beside the green front door. A mat lay across the step, and as June’s shoe touched it, the mat said, quite clearly, in a dry old voice:", "“Wipe.”", "“It talks,” said Arlo.", "“Everything talks. It’s Pendle Stair.” June wiped. Arlo wiped. The mat sniffed, and subsided. “Don’t, um. Don’t answer if it asks about your mother.”", "“Why would it—”", "The door opened.", "It opened inward, all at once, without a hand on it, and a gust of warm air came out smelling of cinnamon, and starch, and boiled cabbage, and hot iron, and something sweet and burnt. Behind it stood a woman. She was in her early fifties, broad and quick, with a headscarf, a stained apron and a wooden spoon in one fist like a sword, and she had Nana Wren’s eyes. They were bright and brown and sharp. They were red around the rims.", "She did not say anything.", "She looked at June for what seemed like a very long time. The spoon came slowly down. And then, with an awful effort, as if lifting something that weighed more than she did, the woman held out both arms.", "June did not run. For a moment she stood quite stiff on the step, with her hands at her sides, and her chin up, and he saw her face working in the effort of holding everything in. “I’m all right,” she said, in a thin, careful, grown-up voice. “I’m sorry I’m late, Aunt Pippa. I’ve had a sort of a busy night. I want you to know I did everything she said, I, and I didn’t cry on the way, not once, I only—”", "“Oh, you silly girl,” said Aunt Philippa, and her voice broke right down the middle, “come here.”", "And June folded.", "It was the worst thing Arlo had ever seen, and he looked away at once. She went into those arms as a house goes down in a landslide, all at once and from the bottom. There was a sound he would never forget. He stared hard at the doormat. The doormat, for once, said nothing at all.", "It was some time before anyone remembered him.", "“And who,” said a dry voice at his elbow, “is this?”", "He looked up. In the doorway behind the aunt there had appeared a girl of about fourteen. She was long and thin and expensively bored, with a sheet of black hair, a great deal of silver on her ears, and her arms crossed in the manner of someone to whom nothing at all had ever been a surprise. She was examining him as one might examine a spider in the bathtub.", "“Is that a human?” she said, to nobody. “It’s very small. Does it bite?”", "“Imogen,” said Aunt Philippa, over June’s head, in a muffled voice. “Be civil.”", "“I’m being civil. I’m asking if it bites.”", "“I don’t bite,” said Arlo.", "“That’s what it would say.”", "“I’m, my name’s Arlo,” said Arlo. “I came with June. Nana sent us. She, um. I’m very sorry about your, about Nana.” He swallowed. “She was nice to me. She made the teapot walk.”", "It was the wrong thing to say, or the right one. The aunt’s head came up. She looked at him over June’s shoulder, with her eyes streaming and her wooden spoon trembling, and for one long moment he thought she was going to be angry. And then, to his enormous surprise, she laughed.", "It was a short wet helpless bark, and she clapped a hand to her mouth.", "“Oh, she did,” said Philippa Wren. “She always did. Forty years I’ve been telling her not to do it in front of guests.” She wiped her face with the back of her wrist, leaving a smear of flour. “Come in, love. Come in, both of you. We’ve been holding supper.”", "“Who’s ‘we’?” said Arlo.", "At which point the house exploded.", "It was a very pleasant explosion. All at once there were a great many people in the hall. There was a short round man with a floury mustache, who turned out to be Uncle Bram, and who shook his hand for an uncomfortably long time without saying a word. There was an enormous old woman in a wheeled chair under a blanket with a cat on it, who was Great-Aunt Marguerite, and who was extremely deaf and extremely rude and who looked him up and down and shouted, to the entire street, “IS THAT THE BOY? HE’S SMALLER THAN I", "Expected. does he eat?” There was a boy of about eleven with bright eyes and a gap in his teeth, who was named Tobias and who had a very small gray rain cloud following him around the ceiling on a string. It drizzled gently on Uncle Bram, who did not seem to notice, and on Arlo, who did.", "“Sorry,” said Tobias. “That’s Mildred. She’s a bit emotional.”", "“Is she a pet?”", "“She’s a cloud.” Tobias was gazing at Arlo’s sneakers in a kind of reverence. “Are those from the human world? Do you have those every day? Can I touch them? What’s the tape do?”", "“Tobias.”", "“I was only asking.”", "The staircase, which was wide enough for one person, moved a little to the left to let them all by. Arlo noticed it, and noticed that nobody else had noticed it, and put it away at the back of his mind, with the mat and the clouds and the three hundred steps, under a heading that was getting larger by the minute. Things It Would Have Been Nice To Be Told."], "nx": "c6s3"}, "c6s3": {"id": "c6s3", "c": 6, "title": "The Wrens", "p": ["He had expected to be an outsider. He had not expected that it would be so much fun.", "Supper was in the kitchen, which was the biggest room in a house of rooms that did not seem to know how big they were. It was low, round, and warm, with a stove the size of a locomotive, and a long scrubbed table surrounded by eleven chairs, none of which matched. A wooden spoon stirred a pot on the stove with nobody holding it. A ladle was handing out soup. A row of copper pans sang along, in a vague and slightly flat chorus, to a tune from a radio on the shelf. Mildred the cloud hung over the table, dripping thoughtfully into the salt.", "He did not know where to sit.", "It was always the worst part. He had perfected the art of arriving somewhere and standing in the exact spot that no one wanted to sit. He stood against the wall by the coats, with his hands folded in front of him, while the family arranged itself. June was at the far end between Aunt Philippa and Uncle Bram, with her face blotched and her eyes swollen, and a bowl of soup in front of her that she was looking at as if it were a very difficult sum. Imogen was draping herself on a chair. Great-Aunt Marguerite was demanding, at the top of her voice, to know whether the boy was a vegetarian.", "“Arlo,” said June, from the other end of the table. “Come here. I saved you a seat.”", "He had not seen her save one. But there it was, a stool, wedged in at her elbow, with its three legs tucked under it like an embarrassed child. He crossed the room in a kind of daze and sat.", "“Thank you,” he whispered.", "“It’s only a stool.”", "“I know.” He looked at his soup. “It’s a good one.”", "It was a very good soup. It was thick and golden, with dumplings that bobbed to the surface and sank again, and he ate it carefully, the way he had eaten the cake, a spoonful at a time, until the plate-sized hole in his stomach began to fill. Nobody told him to hurry. Nobody told him to be neat. A dumpling landed in his bowl without anyone having served it, and when he looked up, Uncle Bram, at the other end of the table, was studying the ceiling with a look of great innocence.", "“Now, tell it,” said Aunt Philippa, at last, when the bowls were empty.", "June told it.", "It took longer than it had with the Registrar, because there were more people to cry. Aunt Philippa did not cry. She sat with her hands folded on the table, and her mouth very straight, and her eyes on her niece, and when June had finished, she was quiet for a long time. Then she got up, very briskly, and went to the pantry, and began to move the jars about. They were perfectly arranged already. She moved them anyway. She took them down one by one and put them back, shifting them an inch to the left, an inch to the right.", "“She does that,” whispered Tobias, in Arlo’s ear. “When there’s something awful. Last year when Grandpa died she did the entire attic.”", "“And Mom?” said June.", "It was a very small word. It dropped into the kitchen like a coin into a well. Aunt Philippa’s hands stopped on a jar of pickled beets.", "“Mom’s,” said June, with a terrible tremor, “still, there hasn’t been, you’d have said. If she was, you’d have told me. Wouldn’t you? If anyone’d heard anything.”", "“Oh, June.”", "“Wouldn’t you?”", "“No, sweetheart. No one’s heard a thing.” Philippa put the jar down. She did not turn. “It’s been three years. Nana took you out of the city the night after, as you know. She thought it was safest. She thought if whatever took your mother came back for the family, it would take longer to find a girl in a school in the human world than a girl on Lantern Row.” Her voice was quite level. “I thought she was being dramatic. I told her so. It’s one of the last things I said to her. I said, Mother, you’re being dramatic.”", "“She wasn’t.”", "“No,” said Philippa Wren, to the jar of beets. “She never was. That was the most irritating thing about her.”", "Nobody said anything.", "Then, from the head of the table, in a hoarse and thundering bellow that made the copper pans jump: “WHAT’S SHE SAYING? HAS SOMEBODY DIED?”", "“It’s all right, Aunt Marguerite,” said Uncle Bram. He patted her hand.", "“Well, somebody has. I’M NOT DEAF. I CAN TELL FROM THE DISHES.” The old woman’s eyes, which were small and pale and extremely sharp, went round the table and landed on Arlo, and stayed. “YOU. BOY.", "Come HERE.”", "He looked at June. June shrugged helplessly. He got up. He crossed the kitchen to the wheeled chair, with the sensation of a person walking toward a very large dog, and stopped a respectful distance away. The cat on the old woman’s blanket opened one eye. It was exactly the color of the Registrar’s glass.", "“CLOSER.”", "He took a step.", "She put out a hand. It was huge, spotted and knotted like a root, and it took his chin, quite gently, in thumb and forefinger, and turned his face to the light. She looked at it for a long time. She looked into his eyes. Something moved far back in her own, a long way down, like a fish turning under the ice.", "“Hmm,” said Great-Aunt Marguerite.", "“Is something wrong?” whispered Arlo.", "“I’M NOT SURE.” She let him go. “YOU’VE GOT A LOOK.”", "“What kind of look?”", "“Like a DOOR,” she said, very loudly, “THAT SOMEBODY’S ABOUT", "To knock on.” And she sat back in her chair and picked up her soup, and did not say another word for the rest of the evening."], "nx": "c6s4"}, "c6s4": {"id": "c6s4", "c": 6, "title": "Lanterns for Odette", "p": ["They sent her off at dusk, on the roof.", "It was the custom of Pendle Stair. When one of the Wrens died, you did not bury them in the ground. You waited for the evening, and you went up to the roof garden, where a thousand years of Wrens had grown rosemary in cracked pots and kept a small iron brazier lit. And there, each member of the family wrote a thing on a paper lantern, and lit it from the flame, and let it go.", "Arlo had never seen a funeral. He had seen the end of one, once, from a bus window: a line of black cars, and a woman in a hat. He had expected something dark. It was not dark at all.", "It was gold.", "The roof garden was long and narrow and a little crooked, hung with strings of glowing glass beads like tiny planets, and the whole of Pendle Stair seemed to be up here, and more. They had come up the stairs in twos and threes as the sun went down. Neighbors in aprons. A man with a violin. A woman in a mail carrier’s cap, with a bag still over her shoulder. A knot of very old ladies in black hats, who all, he noticed, were also carrying wooden spoons. Mildred the cloud had been told firmly to be sensible, and was making herself as small and gray and respectful as possible, hovering a foot above Tobias’s head. And as the light went from rose to violet, one by one, the small paper lanterns were lit.", "They were like pale moons. They rose off the roof in slow soft clouds. He watched a lantern come off a neighboring roof, and then a dozen, and then a hundred more from every island and every bridge, until all of Highmere, from the lowest hump of Underbridge to the tall pale candle of the Hearthcourt, was breathing out lanterns into the dusk.", "“They’re from everywhere,” he said.", "“She was well known,” said June.", "She was standing at the parapet, in a borrowed black dress that was a size too big, with the wind taking the hem of it. She had washed her face. Her braid was neat again. In her hands she held a lantern, a blank white paper one, and a pencil, and she had been looking at them for ten minutes without writing anything.", "“What do you put?”", "“Whatever you like.”", "“I don’t know what to put.”", "“Nobody does. That’s why we’ve got the pencils.” She tried to smile. “You’re supposed to put something true. A little thing. Not ‘she was wonderful.’ Not ‘we’ll miss her.’ You put, ‘she hid the good cookies in the umbrella stand.’”", "“Did she?”", "“In the blue one. Always.” June’s voice shook. She bent over the lantern and wrote, quickly, in a small tight hand. When she straightened, he saw the words on the side of the paper in the evening light. She said I was the best witch on the street.", "“Oh,” said Arlo, and could say nothing else.", "“Here.” Aunt Philippa had come up behind them. She put a lantern in his hands. It was a plain one, of white paper, round as a plum, with a small candle fixed inside, and for a moment he did not understand what it was for. “It’s yours. You’ll want to say something. She’d have liked it.”", "“I don’t, I’m not family.”", "“She sent you. That’s as good.” Philippa Wren looked down at him, with her red eyes and her flour-smeared cheek, and the wooden spoon, which she had apparently brought up here too, tucked into her belt. “Put something small.”", "He stood a long time with the lantern. He thought about everything he had to say. He had known her for two days. He thought of a teapot floating across a room, and a wicked chipped smile, and a small dry hand on the back of his head. In the end, with the stub of June’s pencil, in the best handwriting he had, he wrote only what he was sure of.", "She said I was brave.", "It looked very small on the paper. He did not know if it was right. He held the lantern up toward the brazier, where all the rest were lighting theirs, and waited for his turn.", "The brazier was an iron bowl on three legs, full of a low, steady, ordinary gold flame. People were lifting it off with long wooden tapers. When it was his turn, a girl handed him one. It was a dry thin stick, like a skinny breadstick, and he held it in the fire until it caught. He was careful. He was so careful. He carried it, with both hands, across a foot of air to the wick of the candle in the lantern.", "The flame went out.", "He did not know how it happened. One moment it was a bright little bead at the end of the stick, and the next it was gone, with a faint gray wisp of smoke, as if someone had leaned over and breathed on it. “Oh,” he said. “Sorry.” He went back. He lit it again. He walked back across the roof, in a silence he could suddenly feel on the back of his neck, and it went out again, a foot from the wick.", "“It’s the wind,” said somebody kindly.", "“There isn’t any wind,” said Imogen.", "He tried a third time. It did not even get to the candle. The moment he turned from the brazier, the little flame leaned away from him, the way a flame leans from a drafty window, all the way over, flat and thin and trembling, and snuffed itself. He could feel people looking. There were a hundred faces on this roof, and he could feel every one of them as a small weight on his skin. He stood with the dead taper in his hand and the lantern at his feet and the sensation of an enormous door closing.", "It was the shutters. It was the classroom. It was some people have no purpose in a magical world, child.", "“Even the fire doesn’t want me,” he said, and it came out smaller than he had meant it to.", "“Don’t be silly,” said June, at his elbow, quickly. “It’s only a, it’s only a, you’re nervous. You’re not used to the flame, it can tell. Try again. Try—”", "Something moved in his pocket.", "It was a small, familiar, stubborn kind of movement, a tickle against his hip. He felt the pencil tin rattle. He felt the lid lift, quite by itself, with the dignity of a creature who had been listening to a very long speech and was done with it. And a green thread, thin as sewing silk and no longer than a finger, unwound out of his pocket and down his leg, and went, in the most unhurried way, across the roof.", "“Bartholomew—” Arlo whispered.", "It went up the side of the lantern. It reached the wick. It wound itself, with great tenderness, around the little black thread of it, and then, as everyone on the roof watched, in a silence as deep as water, it did a thing that Arlo had never seen a leaf do.", "It lit.", "It did not catch fire. It kindled. There was a spark in the wick that was not gold. It was white at the heart, and around the white there was a deep clean flowing blue, the blue of the bottom of a swimming pool, the blue of a gas burner turned very low. It spread into the lantern with a soft, deep, steady sound, a kind of breathing, and the paper lit from inside until it glowed like the window of a cathedral. The light fell over his hands. It fell over the faces nearest. It lay in a pool on the old stones of the roof, and everything it touched seemed, for the length of a breath, to be a long way off and very, very old.", "Nobody spoke.", "The lantern lifted out of his hands.", "He had not let go. It simply went. It rose off his palms with a slow, grand, almost courteous motion, and as it passed the brazier, the gold fire in the bowl bent toward it, and then, all at once, in a long soft rustle like a field of wheat in a wind, every flame in the roof garden leaned the same way. Every candle in every lantern. Every glass bead on every string. They all bowed, together, toward the single blue light drifting up into the dusk, as a congregation bows when the door opens and a very great person comes in.", "“That’s not a Wren color,” said Imogen, in a whisper, and for once she did not sound bored.", "“Nobody,” said Aunt Philippa. Her voice was very low and very even, and it carried to every corner of the roof. “Nobody say a word.”", "The blue lantern rose.", "It went up through the whole river of gold, past all the other lanterns, higher than any of them, until it was a spark, a star, a point of cold fire above the bridges and the chimneys and the rooftops of the city. It went into the long low bank of cloud that lay above Highmere in the last of the light. And for a moment, just for the span of a breath, as it passed behind the cloud, the whole slow gray billow of it was lit from within, in a long, lazy, glimmering curve. It looked like a coil. It looked like the side of something enormous, turning, in a very deep sea.", "Then it was a cloud again.", "Arlo found that his hands were shaking. He looked at them. They were perfectly ordinary. They had a scab on the left thumb, and a smear of dirt, and a small pale scar from the wire on the observatory gate. He looked up and found June looking at him with her mouth open, and Aunt Philippa looking at him with a face he could not read, and every single person on the roof looking at him, as one looks at a house where a window, quite without warning, has been lit.", "“I didn’t do that,” he said, in a very small voice. “I swear. It was the leaf.”", "“Of course it was,” said Philippa Wren, slowly.", "But she was not looking at the leaf."], "nx": "c6s5"}, "c6s5": {"id": "c6s5", "c": 6, "title": "The Man at the Back", "p": ["He was standing quite alone, at the very back of the roof.", "It was the first thing Arlo noticed when the murmuring began again, and people turned and spoke, and Uncle Bram started a hymn on the violin in a trembling voice, to bring the evening back to something that could be stood. A man was standing by the stair-head, outside the circle of light. He was tall and spare and very well dressed, in a long gray coat with a high collar and a silver pin at the throat, and he had a beautifully kept gray beard and the pleasant, thoughtful, handsome face of the kind of man who gives out the prizes at school. He wore the mark of the Hearthcourt on his lapel: a small gold flame, in a circle of seven.", "He was the only person on the roof who had not been looking at the lantern.", "He had been looking at Arlo.", "It was not a rude look. It was patient, and warm, and faintly sad, the look of a man who has found the one in the crowd he came for. On his gloved hand, quite still, there sat a small black moth. It had been there for some time. As Arlo watched, the man lifted his hand a little, as if admiring it, and the moth opened its wings. They were as black as ink and had something written on them in tiny silver script. The man read it. His expression did not change. It was only that, after a moment, he began, slowly and quite pleasantly, to smile.", "“Who’s that?” whispered Arlo.", "June, beside him, had seen the lapel pin. She had gone quite still.", "“A councilor,” she said. “One of the Seven. That’s Lysander Thorne. He’s the Keeper of the Quiet. He looks after the seal on the Tanglewood.” Her voice dropped. “She never liked him. But everybody does. He’s very, he sends out presents at midwinter.”", "Lysander Thorne closed his hand. The moth was gone. He tucked something into his sleeve, and he reached into the pocket of his coat, and drew out a small flat box, no bigger than a deck of cards, wrapped in silver paper with a thin black ribbon. And then, with the air of a man arriving late at a party and delighted to find the host, he began to cross the roof.", "People moved aside for him. They did it without noticing, and politely. He had that way of walking. A hand went out here and there for him to shake, and he took each one, with a word and a warm look, and not once did his eyes leave the small figure by the brazier. He came closer. He stopped about three feet away. The gold light of the brazier lay along his beard.", "“Arlo Pruitt,” said Councilor Lysander Thorne.", "It was a very kind voice. It was a voice that had comforted a great many bereaved people at a great many funerals, and Arlo felt, as he heard it, the strange small tug under his ribs of a thing that wanted very much to be good.", "“Yes, sir,” he said.", "“I’m so sorry to meet you in such a sad hour. I knew Odette a long time.” Thorne bowed his head, with its neat gray hair. “I was at the Wardhouse when you came in this morning. I’m afraid I’ve been something of a fool. I ought to have come at once and welcomed you, and instead I was shut up in a very dull meeting about bridges.” His smile was warm and a little rueful. “So I thought I’d make it up to you. It’s only a small thing. A welcome to Highmere.”", "He held out the box.", "It lay on his flat palm, small, silver, and shining, with its black ribbon trailing. In the light of the brazier it looked like the sort of thing a person might have on a shelf for fifty years and never open. It looked, in every respect, like a gift.", "Behind Arlo, on the roof, the gold lanterns went on rising, one by one, into the dark.", "“Go on,” said Councilor Thorne, gently. “Take it. It’s quite free.”"], "nx": "c7s1"}, "c7s1": {"id": "c7s1", "c": 7, "title": "Quite Free", "p": ["“Quite free,” said Councilor Thorne, for the second time.", "Arlo noticed that. In his experience, true things got said once, quickly, by somebody looking at their shoes. It was the other kind that came with a bow on.", "The box lay on the man’s flat palm, silver and small, with its black ribbon trailing in the heat off the brazier. It was exactly the size of the thing he had wanted most for most of his life, which was a present. Not a hand-me-down. Not a jacket with someone else’s name inside the collar. A thing wrapped on purpose, by someone who had been thinking about him while they did it. He had a page for this in the notebook under his mattress. Under P: Presents (none). He could feel his hands beginning to curl toward it all on their own, the way a plant leans toward a window, and he put them behind his back and held one with the other.", "In his pocket, the pencil tin had gone completely still.", "That was how he knew. Bartholomew showed off in front of teachers and Registrars and a whole Council of dignitaries. He had never in his life been quiet on purpose. But the tin was not rattling or leaning or making its small smug noises. It was shut, and flat, and silent, in the way a hamster is silent in a recycling closet when a door opens that it does not like the sound of.", "A hand came to rest on his sleeve. It did not pull. June had not said a word. She was only there, a half-step behind his shoulder, gripping a fold of his sweater, and her hand was shaking."], "nx": "c7s1b"}, "c7s1b": {"id": "c7s1b", "c": 7, "title": "Quite Free", "p": ["“I don’t think I’m allowed,” said Arlo.", "“I beg your pardon?”", "“To take things. From people I don’t know.” He swallowed. “My foster mom says. It’s a rule.”", "“Ah,” said Lysander Thorne, and smiled, a deep, warm, entirely unoffended smile. “What a sensible woman.”", "“Not really,” said Arlo, before he could stop himself.", "Somebody behind him made a noise that was almost a laugh and turned it into a cough. It sounded a good deal like Imogen. Thorne’s eyebrows rose a very little, in a pleased, interested way, and Arlo felt his own ears start to burn.", "“I knew Odette Wren for thirty years,” said the councilor, gently. “She would have wanted me to do this. I think you know that.”", "“I didn’t know her thirty years.”", "“No.”", "“I knew her two days. So I don’t know what she’d have wanted.” His voice was going thin, and he made it stop. “I’m sorry. No, thank you.”", "It was very quiet on the roof.", "The violin had stopped. Uncle Bram, with the bow still lifted, looked from the boy to the councilor, and back again, like a man watching a tennis match he had not meant to buy tickets for. Somewhere to the left, a lantern on a string knocked against a glass bead with a small clear tink.", "Then Councilor Thorne laughed.", "It was a good laugh. It was low, and rueful, and kind, and Arlo thought, with a sort of horror, that he would have liked it very much if it had come from anyone else. “Well!” said Thorne. “Not many people say no to me. I put it down to the beard.” He closed his fingers over the box, and for one half-second, no longer, his gloved hand clenched on it, hard, the way a hand does on a rope that is being pulled the other way. Then it opened again. “Of course. Quite right. I should have thought. A boy who’s lost his friend’s grandmother on the same day he lost everything else, and here comes a stranger with a parcel.”", "“That’s not—”", "“I’ll leave it, then. In case.” He set the box down on the parapet beside the brazier, between two pots of rosemary, with a neat little click, and straightened the black ribbon with one fingertip. “Or for the family. For Odette. It seems a shame to carry it home.” He bowed, to Arlo, to June, to the roof. “My sincere condolences, Mistress Wren. The Council will be wanting to speak with your young friend. In the morning, I should think. Not tonight.”", "He went down the stairs. A hand went out here and there for him to shake, on the way, and he took every one.", "It was only when the sound of his boots had quite died away that Arlo noticed the lantern. It was the blue one, the one that had gone up from his hands. It was a spark now, small and far, above the bridges. And he understood, with a slow, cold, tipping feeling, that the councilor had walked the whole length of that roof, with a hundred people staring at the sky, and had never once looked up at it."], "nx": "c7s2"}, "c7s2": {"id": "c7s2", "c": 7, "title": "Someone Should Have Said", "p": ["He meant to tell them.", "He really did. He told June first, in the stair-head, in a fast whisper, with his back against the wall. It all came out in the wrong order again: the moth, and the gloved hand, and the silver script on the wings, and the way the man had never once looked at the sky. June listened with her arms wrapped round her middle and her eyes on the floor.", "“He’s a councilor,” she said, when he stopped. “He’s one of the Seven. He does the midwinter presents. Nana never liked him, but she never liked anyone who gave a speech.”", "“June. The moth.”", "“I know. I saw the moth.” She rubbed her face with her sleeve. “I’ll tell Aunt Pippa. Don’t touch it. Don’t even look at it. Don’t go near the, just don’t.”", "She did tell her. Arlo watched her do it, across the roof, in the gold crowd, and he saw Aunt Philippa listen with her wooden spoon in one hand and Great-Aunt Marguerite’s wheeled chair in the other, while Tobias asked her something about Mildred and a neighbor asked her where the extra chairs went and a woman in a mail carrier’s cap said, quite kindly, that she was so very sorry, so very sorry, and did Philippa need anything, anything at all. Philippa nodded at all of them. She nodded at June, too, and said something short. She put one hand, briefly, on June’s head.", "“Well?” said Arlo, when June came back.", "“She said it’s very rude to open a councilor’s present at a funeral.” June pulled a face. “She said Uncle Bram will put it somewhere high. And that in the morning, when everyone’s not, when it’s not all so much, we’ll go through it properly.” She hesitated. “She’s not, she isn’t ignoring it. It’s only, it’s Nana. It’s everything. She can only hold so many things.”", "That was true. It was the sort of true that was worse than a lie. He watched Uncle Bram, with the violin tucked under one arm, pick up the silver box by the black ribbon, between finger and thumb, the way you pick up a thing that has been dead a while. He watched him carry it down the stairs into the house. He saw the door close behind him.", "He did not say anything. He thought: You could say something now. You could run after him and shout. But it was a funeral. People were crying. A boy who had been in the city for a single day, who had been a stranger and a guest and a nuisance in forty different ways, was going to run across a roof in front of a hundred mourners and say that a member of the Council was a liar, on no evidence at all, on the strength of a leaf that had gone quiet.", "He had been apologizing to furniture all his life. It turned out that you could not shout at anything.", "Near the parapet, in the thinning crowd, he noticed Imogen. She was standing quite alone, with her black hair blown across her face and her arms folded tight, and she was holding a lantern. It was a white paper one. It was blank. She had not written a single word on it. She was looking at it with a flat, empty, concentrated stare, as if she were trying to read something that had been rubbed off. And as he watched, with an expression of cold and absolute contempt, she lit it from the brazier, and let it go, and did not watch it rise.", "“What are you looking at?” she said, without turning her head.", "“Nothing.”", "“Good. Keep doing it.”"], "nx": "c7s3"}, "c7s3": {"id": "c7s3", "c": 7, "title": "The House Makes an Effort", "p": ["The house had made him a room.", "It was Aunt Philippa who showed him. She had come up the stairs behind him with a candle and a towel and a look of such fierce, determined practicality that he had not dared to ask where the others were sleeping. She had led him up and up, past the landing where the staircase moved aside to let them by, past a door with a tiny painted fish on it, and at the very top, under the eaves, she had stopped before a small plain door that had not been there that morning.", "“It does that,” she said, a little apologetically. “Pendle Stair. It grows a room to fit whoever’s staying. It likes to feel useful.” She cleared her throat. “It can be a bit, eager. If you don’t care for it, say so. It sulks, but it gets over it.”", "She opened the door.", "It was a very small room. He saw that at once. It had a bed with a blue quilt that he would have to climb into from the foot. It had a window about the size of a tea tray, with rain on it. One wall was warm to the touch, and when he laid his palm against it, something deep inside gave a long, tired, contented clank.", "He stood in the middle of the floor with the towel in his arms.", "“Is it all right?” said Aunt Philippa. “I told it not to go to any trouble. If it’s too small, we can, there’s the attic, there’s Tobias’s room, he’s got four beds, I don’t know why—”", "“It’s perfect,” said Arlo.", "He heard his own voice. It was not a lie. That was what made his chest hurt. He had been given the whole of a house, nine stories of it, with a staircase that stepped aside for him and a kitchen with eleven chairs. And the house, left to itself, with nobody to tell it what he was, had looked at him and built a closet.", "“Oh, love,” said Aunt Philippa, quietly. She had seen his face. She did not ask. She only stood in the door with the candle for a moment longer, a broad tired woman in a stained apron, and then she said, in a different voice, “The wall’s the boiler flue. It’s a bit noisy. It’s called Ferdinand.”", "“Ferdinand?”", "“He’s very sensitive about it.”", "She went away. He sat on the bed. He put his hand flat on the warm wall, and the pipe in it said whump, in a low, sleepy, companionable voice, and he said, “Hi, Ferdinand,” and then, because there was nobody to hear him, he put his face in the quilt and was quiet for a while.", "June came to the door in her nightgown a little later, with her hair loose, looking about six.", "“It’s a cupboard,” she said.", "“It’s exactly right.”", "She looked at him, and at the window the size of a tea tray, and at the wall with the pipe in it, and she did not say anything for a moment. Then she said, “I’ll take the next one, then.”", "The wall on the far side of the corridor gave a small creak. When he looked, there was a second small door beside his, with a blue ribbon on the knob. It had not been there either.", "“I keep thinking,” said June, from the doorway, in a very thin voice, “that in the morning I’ll tell Nana about it. About the lanterns, and the, and the funny thing with the fire. I’ll go downstairs and she’ll be in the kitchen with the knitting and I’ll say, Nana, you won’t believe—” She stopped. She stood with her hand on the frame, quite still. “Don’t say sorry.”", "“I wasn’t going to.”", "“You were.”", "“I was,” admitted Arlo.", "“Good night, Arlo.”", "“Good night, June.”", "The house, a long way down, settled itself with a groan. Outside the tea-tray window, the lanterns were still going up, one by one, small and gold and faint, over a city that had stopped to say goodbye."], "nx": "c7s4"}, "c7s4": {"id": "c7s4", "c": 7, "title": "Imogen Is Not Bored", "p": ["He woke to a sound he could not name, and lay with his eyes open in the dark.", "Ferdinand was quiet. It was the quiet that had woken him: a particular, hollow, listening kind, like the silence in a classroom when someone has asked a question and everyone knows who is about to be called. Somewhere below him, a long way down, a door shut very softly. He had spent eight years learning to tell the difference between a door that had been shut by accident and a door that had been shut by someone who did not want to be heard.", "He was out of bed before he decided to be.", "The corridor was black. He put out a hand for the wall, and found, instead, a banister, which had not been there. The staircase had moved. It had slid across the landing and put itself, quite deliberately, under his hand, with a small creak of warning that sounded very like this way.", "“Thanks,” he whispered.", "He went down in his socks. He passed the door with the painted fish. He passed another, where someone was snoring in a series of slow thunderous snorts that rattled the plates in a dresser, and he almost went by, and then did not.", "The snoring had stopped.", "“BOY.”", "It was not loud. That was the shock. It was a low, dry, perfectly distinct voice, coming from the other side of a door that stood an inch ajar, and it did not have a single trace of a shout in it. He stopped with his heart in his throat. In the gap, in the very faint light of a single candle, a small pale eye looked out at him.", "“She took it at midnight,” said Great-Aunt Marguerite, very clearly. “Off the high shelf, with the long spoon. She’s on the roof. Go on.”", "“Mrs., I mean, Great-Aunt, I thought you were—”", "“Deaf,” said the voice, and the eye winked, once, and the door closed. A moment later the snoring began again, thunderous and sincere. He stood in the dark for a moment, and then went on.", "The pantry door was open. The stool was by the shelf. On the top shelf, between a jar of pickled beets and a crock of something called Winter Plums, there was a clean square gap in the dust.", "He went up.", "There were a great many stairs, and the house helped with most of them, and the last flight was a plain wooden ladder with a hatch at the top that had been propped open with a flowerpot. A cold, thin, clean wind came down through it, smelling of rosemary and rain. He climbed through into the roof garden on his hands and knees.", "It was dark. The brazier had burned low, to a handful of red coals that lit nothing. The strings of glass beads hung dead and gray. And at the far end, by the parapet, with her back to him and her black hair loose in the wind, a girl in a nightgown and a long coat was sitting cross-legged on the stones with a small silver box open in her lap.", "The black ribbon lay on the ground beside her, quite neat, quite coiled, like something that had been taken off a sleeping animal.", "It was very quiet. There was no wind, after all. The wind had stopped the moment he came out through the hatch. Something black and small sat on the rim of the box, on the edge of the open lid, perfectly still, with its wings folded flat, and he thought for a long, long moment that it was a bit of burnt paper.", "Then it turned its head.", "“I only wanted to see,” said Imogen.", "She did not turn round. Her voice was not bored. It was not cutting, or dry, or anything at all like it had been on the doorstep, with its sheet of silver and its bite. It was a small, hoarse, scraped-out voice, like someone who had been awake a long time and had cried quietly into a sleeve.", "“Imogen,” whispered Arlo. “Put it down.”", "“I’m not doing anything.”", "“It’s from the man. It’s from, it’s his. June said, your mom said—”", "“Mom said in the morning.” She laughed, a short horrible little sound. “Everyone says in the morning. You know she wrote June a letter? Nana. Every week, since they left. And she never wrote me one. Not once.” Her hands tightened on the box. “She taught June the Sunday jar. She took June to the Gate. She said you’re the best witch on the street to a, a level two, and I’ve been doing this since I was six, and she couldn’t even—”", "“Imogen—”", "“I wasn’t going to say yes to anything,” said Imogen. “I’m not stupid. I heard you. I’m not an idiot. I wasn’t going to take it. I only wanted to hear what it said.”", "The moth on the rim of the box spread its wings.", "There was writing on them. It was tiny and silver and very clean, and it moved, flowing slowly from one edge of the wing to the other, like a ribbon of news. In the dark he could not read it. He saw her read it. He saw her whole face go loose and white in the glow, and her mouth open a little, and her eyes go wide and wet and very young.", "“Say that again,” said Imogen, softly, to the box. “About Nana.”", "And out of the silver box, out of the moth, out of the very air above the parapet, in the soft rich amused voice that had followed Arlo through every night of his life since he was five, someone said:", "“Oh, my dear. I would be delighted.”", "Arlo stopped breathing.", "“That your grandmother kept a great many letters she never sent,” said Kael Veyrin, with infinite tenderness. “Thirty-one of them. In a drawer. Addressed to you. She wrote one every year on your birthday and could never quite think what to say. Do sit down, Imogen. It’s a long story, and the best bits are at the end.”", "The moth turned its small black head. It looked past the girl. It looked across the dark roof, and the cold coals, and the dead glass beads, to a small boy in his socks, frozen at the hatch with one hand on a flowerpot.", "“And is that the little one?” said the voice, with a warm, pleased, patient sigh. “Do come out, Arlo. Don’t hover. I wasn’t talking to you.” A pause. “Yet.”"]}},lore:{}};
